(window.QUIZ_CHUNKS=window.QUIZ_CHUNKS||{})["e3b.e0323b8fe7.js"]={"35":[{"id":"sdl35_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the modes of transmission and life cycle entry points of major intestinal helminths, including ingestion of eggs or encysted larvae, and skin penetration by larvae.","batch":1,"stem":"How do Strongyloides stercoralis larvae enter the human host?","choices":{"A":"Penetration of intact skin","B":"Ingestion of eggs in soil","C":"Bite of a sandfly vector","D":"Inhalation of soil dust","E":"Ingestion of raw fish meat"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Strongyloides worms live in soil, and humans are infected when larvae penetrate the skin. The larvae travel in the circulation to the lungs, move up the trachea, and are swallowed. Ingesting eggs from soil is how Ascaris, Trichuris, and pinworm are acquired, not Strongyloides, whose larvae penetrate skin. Raw fish transmits the tapeworm Diphyllobothrium latum, not Strongyloides. Sandfly bites transmit Leishmania, not Strongyloides, whose larvae penetrate skin from soil. Sources: Robbins Ch. 8 (Strongyloidiasis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Strongyloides, Ancylostoma, and Necator enter through the skin, while Enterobius, Ascaris, Toxocara, Trichinella, and Trichuris are ingested (First Aid Microbiology (Nematodes)). COMLEX: Strongyloides occurs sporadically in the United States, including in Appalachia (Robbins Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 1 — duodenum, jejunum, and ileum histology","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Ingesting eggs from soil is how Ascaris, Trichuris, and pinw","E":"Raw fish transmits the tapeworm Diphyllobothrium latum, not ","C":"Sandfly bites transmit Leishmania, not Strongyloides, whose "}},{"id":"sdl35_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the modes of transmission and life cycle entry points of major intestinal helminths, including ingestion of eggs or encysted larvae, and skin penetration by larvae.","batch":1,"stem":"How do humans acquire Ascaris lumbricoides?","choices":{"A":"Ingestion of metacercariae on plants","B":"Ingestion of eggs passed in stool","C":"Ingestion of cysts in pond water","D":"Bites of infected deer flies","E":"Inhalation of larvae in soil dust"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Ascaris is acquired by ingesting eggs from soil contaminated with human stool (A. lumbricoides) or pig stool (A. suum). Metacercariae on water plants transmit liver flukes such as Fasciola, not Ascaris. Sources: Robbins Ch. 8 (Ascariasis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Ascaris is spread fecal-orally, and its knobby-coated oval eggs are seen in feces (First Aid Microbiology (Nematodes)). COMLEX: about a billion people are estimated to be infected with Ascaris (Robbins Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 1 — duodenum, jejunum, and ileum histology","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Metacercariae on water plants transmit liver flukes such as "}},{"id":"sdl35_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the modes of transmission and life cycle entry points of major intestinal helminths, including ingestion of eggs or encysted larvae, and skin penetration by larvae.","batch":1,"stem":"Humans acquire the adult intestinal form of Taenia solium by ingesting what?","choices":{"A":"Eggs in fecally soiled water","B":"Metacercariae on water plants","C":"Cysticerci in undercooked pork","D":"Cercariae in fresh lake water","E":"Eggs in undercooked raw beef meat"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Ingested cysticerci in undercooked pork attach to the intestinal wall and develop into adult tapeworms several meters long. This route completes the life cycle without causing disseminated cysticercosis. Ingesting T. solium eggs produces tissue cysticercosis, not the adult intestinal tapeworm. Metacercariae on water plants transmit flukes, not Taenia solium. Cercariae in fresh water penetrate skin to cause schistosomiasis, not taeniasis. Raw beef transmits Taenia saginata via cysticerci, not T. solium, and eggs are not the infective form in meat. Sources: Robbins Ch. 8 (Cysticercosis and tapeworm infections). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Taenia solium intestinal tapeworm comes from encysted larvae in pork, and cysticercosis comes from eggs in food contaminated with human feces (First Aid Microbiology (Cestodes)). COMLEX: tapeworm proglottids contain both male and female reproductive organs (Robbins Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 1 — duodenum, jejunum, and ileum histology","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Ingesting T. solium eggs produces tissue cysticercosis, not ","B":"Metacercariae on water plants transmit flukes, not Taenia so","D":"Cercariae in fresh water penetrate skin to cause schistosomi","E":"Raw beef transmits Taenia saginata via cysticerci, not T. so"}},{"id":"sdl35_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain how the worm burden in helminth infections is determined and differentiate species capable of autoinfection from those that are not.","batch":1,"stem":"For most intestinal helminths, which factor determines the adult worm burden?","choices":{"A":"Number of infective exposures","B":"Rate of adult worm replication","C":"Host gastric acid output","D":"Duration of fecal egg shedding","E":"Peak blood eosinophil count"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Most intestinal helminths do not multiply to new adults inside the human host, so each adult worm reflects a separately acquired infective egg or larva. Strongyloides is the key exception. Adult worms do not replicate in the human host, so burden depends on the number of exposures. Egg shedding reflects existing burden but does not determine it, since eggs must mature outside the host. Eosinophil count reflects tissue invasion, not the number of adult worms. Sources: Elentra objective framing (Robbins does not state this directly); Robbins Ch. 8 (Strongyloidiasis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Strongyloides hyperinfection reflects accelerated autoinfection in the immunocompromised (First Aid Microbiology (Nematodes)). COMLEX: Ascaris symptoms vary with the burden of worms, and light infections are often asymptomatic (Robbins Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 5 — small-intestine GALT and secretory IgA","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Adult worms do not replicate in the human host, so burden de","D":"Egg shedding reflects existing burden but does not determine","E":"Eosinophil count reflects tissue invasion, not the number of"}},{"id":"sdl35_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain how the worm burden in helminth infections is determined and differentiate species capable of autoinfection from those that are not.","batch":1,"stem":"In strongyloidiasis, what allows the worm burden to rise without new exposure from soil?","choices":{"A":"Adult worms dividing by fission","B":"Eggs hatching in moist soil","C":"Larvae maturing within the host","D":"Cysts forming in skeletal muscle","E":"Proglottids budding off the scolex"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Most Strongyloides larvae pass in stool, but some develop into infective forms within the host, leading to autoinfection. Females in the small intestinal mucosa produce eggs by parthenogenesis. Helminth adults do not divide by fission; Strongyloides burden rises through autoinfection by larvae maturing in the host. Eggs hatching in soil require new exposure, which is exactly what autoinfection bypasses. Cysts in skeletal muscle describe Trichinella, not Strongyloides autoinfection. Proglottids budding from a scolex describe tapeworms, not Strongyloides. Sources: Robbins Ch. 8 (Strongyloidiasis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: rhabditiform Strongyloides larvae are seen in feces (First Aid Microbiology (Nematodes)). COMLEX: immunocompetent hosts with Strongyloides are often asymptomatic but may have diarrhea, bloating, or malabsorption (Robbins Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 5 — small-intestine GALT and secretory IgA","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Helminth adults do not divide by fission; Strongyloides burd","B":"Eggs hatching in soil require new exposure, which is exactly","D":"Cysts in skeletal muscle describe Trichinella, not Strongylo","E":"Proglottids budding from a scolex describe tapeworms, not St"}},{"id":"sdl35_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain how the worm burden in helminth infections is determined and differentiate species capable of autoinfection from those that are not.","batch":1,"stem":"Which drug class both suppresses eosinophil function and stimulates female Strongyloides to produce more infective larvae?","choices":{"A":"Beta blockers","B":"Antihistamines","C":"Statin agents","D":"Loop diuretic agents","E":"Corticosteroids"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Corticosteroids inhibit eosinophils and other immune cells that accumulate in response to infection, and they stimulate female worms to increase larva production. Prolonged therapy can lead to fatal hyperinfection. Corticosteroids, not beta blockers, inhibit eosinophil function and stimulate female Strongyloides to increase infective larva production. Corticosteroids, not antihistamines, inhibit eosinophil function and stimulate female Strongyloides to increase infective larva production. Corticosteroids, not statins, inhibit eosinophil function and stimulate female Strongyloides to increase infective larva production. Sources: Robbins Ch. 8 (Strongyloidiasis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: ivermectin or bendazoles treat Strongyloides (First Aid Microbiology (Nematodes)). COMLEX: organ transplantation and lymphoma also increase the risk of hyperinfection (Robbins Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 5 — small-intestine GALT and secretory IgA","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Corticosteroids, not beta blockers, inhibit eosinophil funct","B":"Corticosteroids, not antihistamines, inhibit eosinophil func","C":"Corticosteroids, not statins, inhibit eosinophil function an"}},{"id":"sdl35_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify clinical manifestations associated with specific intestinal helminths and describe mechanisms of disease including nutrient competition, mechanical obstruction, and mucosal damage.","batch":1,"stem":"How do hookworms cause anemia?","choices":{"A":"Hemolysis of circulating red cells","B":"Blood loss from the intestinal wall","C":"Marrow invasion by migrating larvae","D":"Competition for dietary cobalamin","E":"Malabsorption of dietary folate"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Ancylostoma and Necator attach to the intestinal wall and suck blood, causing chronic blood loss and microcytic anemia. Hookworms do not hemolyze red cells; they suck blood from the intestinal wall, causing iron deficiency. Competition for cobalamin is the mechanism of Diphyllobothrium latum, not hookworm. Sources: First Aid Microbiology (Nematodes) (Robbins Ch. 8 does not cover hookworm; Elentra objective framing). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: iron deficiency anemia is the classic parasite hint for Ancylostoma and Necator (First Aid Microbiology (Nematodes)). COMLEX: hookworm larvae penetrate skin after walking barefoot on contaminated soil (First Aid Microbiology (Nematodes)).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 5 — intestinal absorption of iron, calcium, and fat-soluble vitamins","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Hookworms do not hemolyze red cells; they suck blood from th","D":"Competition for cobalamin is the mechanism of Diphyllobothri"}},{"id":"sdl35_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify clinical manifestations associated with specific intestinal helminths and describe mechanisms of disease including nutrient competition, mechanical obstruction, and mucosal damage.","batch":1,"stem":"Which nutrient deficiency results from Diphyllobothrium latum infection?","choices":{"A":"Vitamin B6","B":"Folic acid","C":"Thiamine","D":"Vitamin B12","E":"Ascorbic acid"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Diphyllobothrium latum competes with the host for vitamin B12 in the intestine, causing megaloblastic anemia. Robbins notes that in humans it lives only in the gut and does not form cysticerci. Folate is not consumed by Diphyllobothrium; it competes for vitamin B12. Diphyllobothrium latum competes with the host for dietary vitamin B12 and can cause megaloblastic anemia; vitamin B6 is not affected. Thiamine deficiency is typical of alcohol use disorder, whereas the fish tapeworm competes for vitamin B12. Sources: First Aid Microbiology (Cestodes); Robbins Ch. 8 (Cysticercosis and tapeworm infections). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Diphyllobothrium latum is acquired by ingesting larvae in raw freshwater fish (First Aid Microbiology (Cestodes)). COMLEX: the beef tapeworm and fish tapeworm are acquired by eating undercooked meat or fish (Robbins Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 5 — intestinal absorption of iron, calcium, and fat-soluble vitamins","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Folate is not consumed by Diphyllobothrium; it competes for ","A":"Diphyllobothrium latum competes with the host for dietary vi","C":"Thiamine deficiency is typical of alcohol use disorder, wher"}},{"id":"sdl35_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify clinical manifestations associated with specific intestinal helminths and describe mechanisms of disease including nutrient competition, mechanical obstruction, and mucosal damage.","batch":1,"stem":"Heavy Ascaris burdens can mechanically obstruct which structure?","choices":{"A":"Ileocecal valve","B":"Pyloric sphincter","C":"Lower esophageal sphincter","D":"Internal anal sphincter","E":"Duodenojejunal flexure"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Heavy Ascaris burdens can cause abdominal obstruction requiring surgery, including obstruction of the ileocecal valve and the appendix. Sources: Robbins Ch. 8 (Ascariasis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 35 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Ascaris may also cause biliary obstruction and intestinal perforation (First Aid Microbiology (Nematodes)). COMLEX: adult Ascaris worms are 15 to 35 cm long and live mainly in the jejunum (Robbins Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 5 — intestinal absorption of iron, calcium, and fat-soluble vitamins","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins"},{"id":"sdl35_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the role of eosinophilia in helminth infections and describe when eosinophilia is expected based on tissue invasion.","batch":1,"stem":"Which Th2 cytokine stimulates eosinophils in helminth infections?","choices":{"A":"Interleukin 2","B":"Interleukin 12","C":"Interleukin 17","D":"Interferon gamma","E":"Interleukin 5"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Invasive helminths stimulate a Th2 response. IL-5 stimulates eosinophils, and IL-4 drives IgE production. IL-12 drives Th1 responses, not the Th2 eosinophil response to helminths. Interferon gamma is a Th1 cytokine, whereas helminths elicit Th2 responses with IL-5. IL-2 is the T-cell growth factor that drives T-cell proliferation, not the cytokine that stimulates eosinophils. IL-17 is the Th17 cytokine that recruits neutrophils and monocytes, not the Th2 eosinophil stimulator. Sources: Robbins Ch. 8 (Trichinellosis); Robbins Ch. 8 (Filariasis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 35 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: eosinophils produce major basic protein and respond to IL-5 (First Aid Hematology (Eosinophils)). COMLEX: Th2 responses increase intestinal contractility, which expels adult Trichinella worms in animal models (Robbins Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 1 — pharmacology and clinical uses of antihelminthic drugs","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins","whyNot":{"B":"IL-12 drives Th1 responses, not the Th2 eosinophil response ","D":"Interferon gamma is a Th1 cytokine, whereas helminths elicit","A":"IL-2 is the T-cell growth factor that drives T-cell prolifer","C":"IL-17 is the Th17 cytokine that recruits neutrophils and mon"}},{"id":"sdl35_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the role of eosinophilia in helminth infections and describe when eosinophilia is expected based on tissue invasion.","batch":1,"stem":"When is peripheral eosinophilia most expected during a helminth infection?","choices":{"A":"When adults reach peak fecundity","B":"When eggs pass in the stool","C":"When worms attach by a scolex","D":"When larvae invade host tissues","E":"When adults reside in the cecum"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Eosinophilia is driven by Th2 responses to parasites that migrate through or encyst in tissue. Trichinella larvae invading muscle cause marked eosinophilia, and Ascaris larvae in the lungs cause eosinophilic pneumonitis. Peak fecundity of luminal adults does not drive eosinophilia, which requires tissue invasion. Eggs in stool reflect luminal adults, which do not cause systemic eosinophilia. Scolex attachment is luminal tapeworm behavior, and luminal tapeworms do not cause eosinophilia. Adults in the cecum, like pinworms, stay luminal and do not cause eosinophilia. Sources: Elentra objective framing (Robbins does not state this directly); Robbins Ch. 8 (Trichinellosis); Robbins Ch. 8 (Ascariasis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: parasites lead the PACMAN mnemonic for causes of eosinophilia (First Aid Hematology (Eosinophils)). COMLEX: granulomas around schistosome eggs contain eosinophils, which are distinctive for helminth infections (Robbins Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 1 — pharmacology and clinical uses of antihelminthic drugs","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Peak fecundity of luminal adults does not drive eosinophilia","B":"Eggs in stool reflect luminal adults, which do not cause sys","C":"Scolex attachment is luminal tapeworm behavior, and luminal ","E":"Adults in the cecum, like pinworms, stay luminal and do not "}},{"id":"sdl35_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the role of eosinophilia in helminth infections and describe when eosinophilia is expected based on tissue invasion.","batch":1,"stem":"Which eosinophil granule product is toxic to helminths?","choices":{"A":"Cathelicidin peptide","B":"Major basic protein","C":"Serum amyloid A","D":"Lysozyme enzyme","E":"Lactoferrin protein"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Eosinophils defend against helminths and release major basic protein, a helminthotoxin, along with eosinophil cationic protein and eosinophil-derived neurotoxin. Cathelicidins are antimicrobial proteins of neutrophils, whereas major basic protein is the eosinophil product toxic to helminths. Serum amyloid A is a hepatic acute-phase protein induced by IL-1 and TNF, not an eosinophil granule product. Lysozyme is a neutrophil granule enzyme, not the eosinophil helminthotoxin major basic protein. Lactoferrin is found in neutrophil specific granules, not eosinophil granules. Sources: First Aid Hematology (Eosinophils); Elentra objective framing (Robbins does not state this directly). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 35 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: eosinophils have bilobate nuclei and large uniform eosinophilic granules (First Aid Hematology (Eosinophils)). COMLEX: corticosteroids inhibit eosinophils that accumulate in tissues in response to Strongyloides (Robbins Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 1 — pharmacology and clinical uses of antihelminthic drugs","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins","whyNot":{"A":"Cathelicidins are antimicrobial proteins of neutrophils, whe","C":"Serum amyloid A is a hepatic acute-phase protein induced by ","D":"Lysozyme is a neutrophil granule enzyme, not the eosinophil ","E":"Lactoferrin is found in neutrophil specific granules, not eo"}},{"id":"sdl35_q25","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the diagnostic findings and appropriate pharmacologic treatments of major intestinal helminth infections, including identification of eggs, larvae, or adult worms and the mechanisms of action of commonly used anthelmintic agents.","batch":1,"stem":"How does pyrantel pamoate eliminate susceptible intestinal nematodes?","choices":{"A":"Inhibition of tubulin polymerization","B":"Increased tegument calcium permeability","C":"Neuromuscular blockade of worms","D":"Inhibition of parasite folate synthesis","E":"Uncoupling of oxidative phosphorylation"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Pyrantel is a poorly absorbed neuromuscular blocking agent that releases acetylcholine and inhibits cholinesterase, paralyzing luminal worms, which are then expelled. Inhibiting tubulin polymerization is the action of benzimidazoles such as albendazole, not pyrantel. Increasing tegument calcium permeability is the action of praziquantel, not pyrantel. Uncoupling of oxidative phosphorylation is the presumed action of niclosamide against tapeworms, whereas pyrantel is a neuromuscular blocking agent. Inhibiting folate synthesis (dihydrofolate reductase) is the action of antiprotozoals such as pyrimethamine, not pyrantel. Sources: Katzung Ch. 53 (Pyrantel Pamoate). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: pyrantel pamoate is listed with ivermectin, mebendazole, and diethylcarbamazine as antihelminthic therapy (First Aid Microbiology (Antihelminthic therapy)). COMLEX: pyrantel is ineffective against Trichuris and Strongyloides infections (Katzung Ch. 53).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 1 — pharmacology and clinical uses of antihelminthic drugs","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Inhibiting tubulin polymerization is the action of benzimida","B":"Increasing tegument calcium permeability is the action of pr","E":"Uncoupling of oxidative phosphorylation is the presumed acti","D":"Inhibiting folate synthesis (dihydrofolate reductase) is the"}},{"id":"sdl35_q26","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the diagnostic findings and appropriate pharmacologic treatments of major intestinal helminth infections, including identification of eggs, larvae, or adult worms and the mechanisms of action of commonly used anthelmintic agents.","batch":1,"stem":"What shape do Enterobius vermicularis eggs have on microscopic examination?","choices":{"A":"Barrel shape with polar plugs","B":"Oval with one flattened side","C":"Round with a thick knobby shell","D":"Oval with a large lateral spine","E":"Operculated with a terminal knob"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Pinworm eggs have a characteristic elongated oval shape with one of the long sides of the oval flattened. Barrel-shaped eggs with polar plugs are Trichuris, not pinworm. Round eggs with a thick knobby (mammillated) shell are Ascaris, not pinworm. Oval eggs with a large lateral spine are Schistosoma mansoni, not pinworm. Operculated eggs with a terminal knob are Diphyllobothrium, not pinworm. Sources: Robbins Ch. 8 (Enterobiasis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: pinworm causes anal pruritus worse at night and is most common in children aged 5 to 10 (First Aid Microbiology (Nematodes)). COMLEX: adult pinworms live in the cecum and appendix, and females migrate out of the anus at night (Robbins Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 1 — pharmacology and clinical uses of antihelminthic drugs","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Barrel-shaped eggs with polar plugs are Trichuris, not pinwo","C":"Round eggs with a thick knobby (mammillated) shell are Ascar","D":"Oval eggs with a large lateral spine are Schistosoma mansoni","E":"Operculated eggs with a terminal knob are Diphyllobothrium, "}},{"id":"sdl35_q27","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the diagnostic findings and appropriate pharmacologic treatments of major intestinal helminth infections, including identification of eggs, larvae, or adult worms and the mechanisms of action of commonly used anthelmintic agents.","batch":1,"stem":"Which drug of choice treats intestinal infection with the fish tapeworm Diphyllobothrium latum?","choices":{"A":"Praziquantel","B":"Ivermectin","C":"Diethylcarbamazine","D":"Pyrantel pamoate","E":"Mebendazole"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Katzung lists praziquantel or niclosamide as the drugs of choice for Diphyllobothrium latum and Taenia saginata, which live only in the gut lumen. Ivermectin is the drug of choice for Strongyloides and Onchocerca, not tapeworms. Diethylcarbamazine treats filarial infections, not intestinal tapeworms. Pyrantel pamoate treats luminal nematodes such as pinworm, Ascaris, and hookworm, not tapeworms. Mebendazole is first-line for intestinal roundworms, whereas praziquantel is the drug of choice for tapeworms. Sources: Katzung Ch. 53 (Table 53-1); Robbins Ch. 8 (Cysticercosis and tapeworm infections). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: tapeworms such as Diphyllobothrium latum are covered with the cestodes (First Aid Microbiology (Cestodes)). COMLEX: a single 2-g dose of niclosamide cures more than 85% of Diphyllobothrium latum infections (Katzung Ch. 53).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 1 — pharmacology and clinical uses of antihelminthic drugs","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Ivermectin is the drug of choice for Strongyloides and Oncho","C":"Diethylcarbamazine treats filarial infections, not intestina","D":"Pyrantel pamoate treats luminal nematodes such as pinworm, A","E":"Mebendazole is first-line for intestinal roundworms, whereas"}},{"id":"sdl35_q13","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the modes of transmission and life cycle entry points of major intestinal helminths, including ingestion of eggs or encysted larvae, and skin penetration by larvae.","batch":2,"stem":"A 30-year-old pig farmer passes a 25-cm worm in his stool. He recalls a dry cough with fever several weeks earlier. After the eggs were ingested, which route did the larvae take before maturing in his intestine?","choices":{"A":"Portal flow to the hepatic lobules","B":"Lymphatic flow to the dermis","C":"Direct growth in the jejunal lumen","D":"Lung passage then reswallowing","E":"Bile flow back into the duodenum"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Ingested Ascaris eggs release larvae in the small intestine. The larvae penetrate the cecal mucosa, travel to the lungs in blood or lymph, mature for 10 to 14 days, and are coughed up and swallowed. Ascaris suum is acquired from pig stool. Ascaris larvae do not mature directly in the lumen; they migrate to the lungs and are reswallowed, unlike Trichuris. Bile flow back into the duodenum is not part of the Ascaris life cycle; larvae migrate through the lungs. Sources: Robbins Ch. 8 (Ascariasis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: migration of Ascaris larvae to alveoli causes Löffler syndrome with pulmonary eosinophilia (First Aid Microbiology (Nematodes)). COMLEX: each female Ascaris releases about 200,000 eggs per day (Robbins Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 1 — duodenum, jejunum, and ileum histology","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Ascaris larvae do not mature directly in the lumen; they mig","E":"Bile flow back into the duodenum is not part of the Ascaris "}},{"id":"sdl35_q14","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the modes of transmission and life cycle entry points of major intestinal helminths, including ingestion of eggs or encysted larvae, and skin penetration by larvae.","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old vegetarian woman who emigrated from rural Guatemala has new seizures. MRI shows multiple small cystic brain lesions. How did she most likely acquire the infection?","choices":{"A":"Larvae in undercooked pork","B":"Metacercariae on watercress","C":"Eggs from dog feces","D":"Cercariae in lake water","E":"Eggs from human feces"},"correct":"E","explanation":"When humans ingest Taenia solium eggs in food or water contaminated with human feces, oncospheres penetrate the gut wall and spread hematogenously to form cysticerci, including in the brain. Adult tapeworms do not form by this route. Larvae in undercooked pork produce the adult intestinal tapeworm, not brain cysticerci, and she is vegetarian. Metacercariae on watercress transmit Fasciola, not cysticercosis. Eggs from dog feces cause echinococcal hydatid cysts, whereas T. solium eggs come from human feces. Cercariae in lake water cause schistosomiasis, not neurocysticercosis. Sources: Robbins Ch. 8 (Cysticercosis and tapeworm infections). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: brain cysts with seizures is the parasite hint for neurocysticercosis (First Aid Microbiology (Cestodes)). COMLEX: viable cysticerci evade immunity with taeniaestatin and paramyosin (Robbins Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 1 — pharmacology and clinical uses of antihelminthic drugs","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Larvae in undercooked pork produce the adult intestinal tape","B":"Metacercariae on watercress transmit Fasciola, not cysticerc","C":"Eggs from dog feces cause echinococcal hydatid cysts, wherea","D":"Cercariae in lake water cause schistosomiasis, not neurocyst"}},{"id":"sdl35_q15","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the modes of transmission and life cycle entry points of major intestinal helminths, including ingestion of eggs or encysted larvae, and skin penetration by larvae.","batch":2,"stem":"A 26-year-old aid worker waded in a freshwater lake in East Africa. Months later she has hepatomegaly and eggs in her stool. Which mechanism allowed the parasite to enter her body?","choices":{"A":"Larval proteolytic enzymes","B":"Ingestion of snail tissue","C":"Bite of an infected black fly","D":"Inhalation of aerosolized eggs","E":"Ingestion of contaminated watercress"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Schistosome cercariae swim in fresh water and penetrate human skin with proteolytic enzymes that degrade the keratinized layer, causing minimal skin reaction. S. mansoni affects mainly the liver and gut. Contaminated watercress transmits Fasciola metacercariae, whereas schistosome cercariae penetrate skin. Black flies transmit Onchocerca volvulus near fast-moving rivers, not schistosomes, whose cercariae penetrate skin in fresh water. Sources: Robbins Ch. 8 (Schistosomiasis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 35 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: portal hypertension is the parasite hint for Schistosoma mansoni and S. japonicum (First Aid Microbiology (Trematodes); Parasite hints). COMLEX: females produce hundreds to thousands of eggs per day (Robbins Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 4 — portal hypertension and portosystemic varices","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins","whyNot":{"E":"Contaminated watercress transmits Fasciola metacercariae, wh","C":"Black flies transmit Onchocerca volvulus near fast-moving ri"}},{"id":"sdl35_q16","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain how the worm burden in helminth infections is determined and differentiate species capable of autoinfection from those that are not.","batch":2,"stem":"A 7-year-old boy has nocturnal perianal itching that returns weeks after one course of treatment. He often sucks his thumb, and no one else at home is affected. What best explains his reinfection?","choices":{"A":"Contaminated drinking water","B":"Hand-to-mouth egg transfer","C":"Adult worm division in cecum","D":"Insect vector bite","E":"Eating undercooked meat"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Pinworm females migrate out of the anus at night to deposit eggs on perianal skin. The eggs become infectious within about 6 hours and spread from the hands or bed linens, allowing self-reinfection. Adult worms do not divide; pinworm reinfection comes from re-ingesting eggs. Undercooked meat transmits tapeworms and Trichinella, not pinworm. Sources: Robbins Ch. 8 (Enterobiasis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Enterobius is spread fecal-orally and diagnosed with the tape test (First Aid Microbiology (Nematodes)). COMLEX: pinworm is the most common helminthic infection in the United States (Robbins Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 5 — defecation reflex and the anal sphincters","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Adult worms do not divide; pinworm reinfection comes from re","E":"Undercooked meat transmits tapeworms and Trichinella, not pi"}},{"id":"sdl35_q17","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain how the worm burden in helminth infections is determined and differentiate species capable of autoinfection from those that are not.","batch":2,"stem":"A 62-year-old man from rural Kentucky takes high-dose prednisone after a lung transplant. He develops diffuse pulmonary infiltrates and Escherichia coli bacteremia, and larvae are found in his sputum. What is the source of the bacteremia?","choices":{"A":"Contaminated central venous line","B":"Aspiration of gastric contents","C":"Larval damage to the bowel wall","D":"Infected urinary catheter","E":"Obstructed common bile duct"},"correct":"C","explanation":"In Strongyloides hyperinfection, larvae invade the intestinal wall, and intestinal bacteria enter the blood through the damaged wall, causing sepsis. Worms of all stages may be found in the lungs and sputum. Sources: Robbins Ch. 8 (Strongyloidiasis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 35 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Strongyloides causes pulmonary symptoms such as dry cough and hemoptysis (First Aid Microbiology (Nematodes)). COMLEX: hyperinfection larvae invade the colonic submucosa, lymphatics, and blood vessels (Robbins Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 4 — small-intestine epithelial renewal","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins"},{"id":"sdl35_q18","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain how the worm burden in helminth infections is determined and differentiate species capable of autoinfection from those that are not.","batch":2,"stem":"A 51-year-old man from Jamaica with no history of immunosuppressive drug use develops overwhelming strongyloidiasis with larvae throughout his body. Which underlying infection most likely predisposed him?","choices":{"A":"Chronic hepatitis C virus","B":"Latent Epstein-Barr virus","C":"High-risk papillomavirus","D":"Reactivated human cytomegalovirus","E":"Human T-lymphotropic virus 1"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Human T-lymphotropic virus type I infection predisposes to very high Strongyloides worm burdens through uncontrolled autoinfection, leading to fatal disease. Chronic hepatitis C (with hepatitis B) virus infection is a cause of hepatocellular carcinoma, not the infection that predisposes to Strongyloides hyperinfection. Epstein-Barr virus is implicated in Burkitt lymphoma, nasopharyngeal carcinoma, and B-cell lymphomas in T-cell immunosuppression, not in Strongyloides hyperinfection. High-risk HPV types such as 16 and 18 cause squamous cell carcinomas of the cervix, anogenital region, and head and neck, not overwhelming strongyloidiasis. Latent cytomegalovirus reactivates when cellular immunity is depressed and then causes systemic CMV disease; it is not the classic predisposition to Strongyloides hyperinfection. Sources: Robbins Ch. 8 (Strongyloidiasis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 35 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: hyperinfection syndrome results from accelerated autoinfection in the immunocompromised (First Aid Microbiology (Nematodes)). COMLEX: Strongyloides is endemic in tropical South America, sub-Saharan Africa, and Southeast Asia (Robbins Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 2 — choosing among antihelminthic drugs","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins","whyNot":{"A":"Chronic hepatitis C (with hepatitis B) virus infection is a ","B":"Epstein-Barr virus is implicated in Burkitt lymphoma, nasoph","C":"High-risk HPV types such as 16 and 18 cause squamous cell ca","D":"Latent cytomegalovirus reactivates when cellular immunity is"}},{"id":"sdl35_q19","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify clinical manifestations associated with specific intestinal helminths and describe mechanisms of disease including nutrient competition, mechanical obstruction, and mucosal damage.","batch":2,"stem":"A 9-year-old girl in rural Colombia passed a long roundworm last month. She now has severe epigastric pain radiating to the back, and imaging shows a worm in the common bile duct. Which complication has developed?","choices":{"A":"Acute pancreatitis","B":"Perforated duodenal ulcer","C":"Acute mesenteric ischemia","D":"Splenic infarction","E":"Ureteral colic"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Ascaris worms that enter the biliary tree can cause acute pancreatitis and cholangitis. Sources: Robbins Ch. 8 (Ascariasis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Ascaris may cause biliary obstruction and can emerge from the nose or mouth (First Aid Microbiology (Nematodes)). COMLEX: Ascaris infections are diagnosed by eggs in the stool or passed adult worms (Robbins Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 2 — acute and chronic pancreatitis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl35_q20","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify clinical manifestations associated with specific intestinal helminths and describe mechanisms of disease including nutrient competition, mechanical obstruction, and mucosal damage.","batch":2,"stem":"A 10-year-old boy in rural Southeast Asia who walks barefoot has fatigue and pallor. Hemoglobin is 8.2 g/dL (reference 11.5–15.5 g/dL) and MCV is 68 fL (reference 77–95 fL). Stool shows nematode eggs. Which additional laboratory finding is expected?","choices":{"A":"Low serum folate","B":"Low serum ferritin","C":"High indirect bilirubin","D":"High methylmalonic acid","E":"Positive Coombs test"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Hookworms suck blood from the intestinal wall, and chronic blood loss causes iron deficiency with microcytic anemia and depleted iron stores. High indirect bilirubin implies hemolysis, but hookworm anemia comes from chronic blood loss. High methylmalonic acid indicates B12 deficiency, as with fish tapeworm, not the microcytic anemia of hookworm. A positive Coombs test indicates immune hemolysis, not hookworm blood loss. Sources: First Aid Microbiology (Nematodes) (Robbins Ch. 8 does not cover hookworm; Elentra objective framing). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: hookworm can also cause cutaneous larva migrans, a pruritic serpiginous rash (First Aid Microbiology (Nematodes)). COMLEX: hookworm larvae enter through skin after walking barefoot on contaminated beach or soil (First Aid Microbiology (Nematodes)).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 5 — intestinal absorption of iron, calcium, and fat-soluble vitamins","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"High indirect bilirubin implies hemolysis, but hookworm anem","D":"High methylmalonic acid indicates B12 deficiency, as with fi","E":"A positive Coombs test indicates immune hemolysis, not hookw"}},{"id":"sdl35_q21","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify clinical manifestations associated with specific intestinal helminths and describe mechanisms of disease including nutrient competition, mechanical obstruction, and mucosal damage.","batch":2,"stem":"A 7-year-old girl with nightly perianal itching develops vulvar itching and discharge. A tape test is positive. What mechanism explains her new symptoms?","choices":{"A":"Eggs carried in the bloodstream","B":"Larvae entering through the skin","C":"Bacteria spreading from the bladder","D":"Worms moving into the vagina","E":"Cysts forming in the vulvar tissue"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Enterobius adults live in the cecum and appendix, and females migrate out of the anus at night. Worms moving into the vagina can cause vulvovaginitis. Pinworm is acquired by ingesting eggs, not by larvae entering through skin. Sources: Robbins Ch. 8 (Enterobiasis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Enterobius is most common in children aged 5 to 10 years (First Aid Microbiology (Nematodes)). COMLEX: very high pinworm burdens rarely cause rectal prolapse (Robbins Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 5 — appendicitis and anal canal lesions","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Pinworm is acquired by ingesting eggs, not by larvae enterin"}},{"id":"sdl35_q22","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the role of eosinophilia in helminth infections and describe when eosinophilia is expected based on tissue invasion.","batch":2,"stem":"A 44-year-old hunter ate undercooked bear meat 3 weeks ago. He has fever, myalgias, and periorbital edema, and the eosinophil fraction is 32% (reference range: 1–4%). Where are the parasites now located?","choices":{"A":"Skeletal muscle cells","B":"Hepatic sinusoids","C":"Lumen of the cecum","D":"Intrahepatic bile ducts","E":"Renal tubular cells"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Trichinella larvae disseminate hematogenously and penetrate striated muscle cells, causing fever, myalgias, marked eosinophilia, and periorbital edema. The infected muscle cell gains a collagenous capsule and new blood vessels. Trichinella adults live briefly in the intestine, but by 3 weeks the larvae have encysted in skeletal muscle. Sources: Robbins Ch. 8 (Trichinellosis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 35 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Trichinella larvae encyst in striated muscle after undercooked pork ingestion (First Aid Microbiology (Nematodes)). COMLEX: humans are dead-end hosts for Trichinella (Robbins Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 1 — pharmacology and clinical uses of antihelminthic drugs","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins","whyNot":{"C":"Trichinella adults live briefly in the intestine, but by 3 w"}},{"id":"sdl35_q23","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the role of eosinophilia in helminth infections and describe when eosinophilia is expected based on tissue invasion.","batch":2,"stem":"A 5-year-old boy has dry cough, wheezing, fever, and transient pulmonary infiltrates with eosinophilia. Two stool examinations show no eggs, but 2 months later he passes a large roundworm. Why were the early stool tests negative?","choices":{"A":"Eggs were destroyed by acid","B":"Larvae had not yet matured","C":"Worms were in the bile duct","D":"Infection was in the skin","E":"Eggs were shed in urine"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The initial pulmonary stage of ascariasis causes Loeffler syndrome, a self-limited eosinophilic pneumonitis. Stool examination may not reveal the parasites if they have not yet matured in the intestine. Ascaris is acquired by ingesting eggs, so it has no skin phase. Sources: Robbins Ch. 8 (Ascariasis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Löffler syndrome is pulmonary eosinophilia from Ascaris larvae in the alveoli (First Aid Microbiology (Nematodes)). COMLEX: heavy Ascaris burdens can cause malnutrition (Robbins Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 2 — choosing among antihelminthic drugs","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"Ascaris is acquired by ingesting eggs, so it has no skin pha"}},{"id":"sdl35_q24","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the role of eosinophilia in helminth infections and describe when eosinophilia is expected based on tissue invasion.","batch":2,"stem":"A 40-year-old Ethiopian man who eats raw beef passes motile flat segments in his stool. The eosinophil fraction is 3% (reference range: 1–4%). What best explains the normal eosinophil count?","choices":{"A":"Steroid suppression of eosinophils","B":"Concurrent bacterial sepsis","C":"Adult worm confined to gut lumen","D":"Eosinophils trapped in spleen","E":"Low parasite egg output"},"correct":"C","explanation":"In humans, Taenia saginata lives only in the gut lumen and does not form tissue cysticerci, so it lacks the tissue phase that drives strong Th2 eosinophilic responses. He has no steroid exposure; his normal count reflects a luminal adult worm without tissue invasion. Egg output does not drive eosinophilia; tissue invasion does. Sources: Robbins Ch. 8 (Cysticercosis and tapeworm infections); Elentra objective framing (Robbins does not state this directly). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: parasites are a classic cause of eosinophilia in the PACMAN mnemonic (First Aid Hematology (Eosinophils)). COMLEX: tapeworm mature distal proglottids contain many eggs and detach into the feces (Robbins Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 5 — small-intestine GALT and secretory IgA","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"He has no steroid exposure; his normal count reflects a lumi","E":"Egg output does not drive eosinophilia; tissue invasion does"}},{"id":"sdl35_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the diagnostic findings and appropriate pharmacologic treatments of major intestinal helminth infections, including identification of eggs, larvae, or adult worms and the mechanisms of action of commonly used anthelmintic agents.","batch":2,"stem":"A 7-year-old girl has 10 days of perianal itching that wakes her at night. A morning tape test is positive for pinworm eggs. Her CBC is normal, with hemoglobin 12.6 g/dL (reference range: 11.5–15.5 g/dL). Which treatment plan is most appropriate?","choices":{"A":"Mebendazole now and at 2 weeks","B":"Niclosamide as one single dose","C":"Oral ivermectin daily for 2 days","D":"Oral metronidazole for 7 days","E":"Oral vancomycin given for 10 days"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Tape test eggs flattened on one side confirm Enterobius. Katzung gives mebendazole 100 mg once for pinworm, repeated at 2 weeks, because reinfection from newly hatched worms is common. Niclosamide treats tapeworms, not pinworm. Ivermectin is the drug of choice for Strongyloides, not pinworm, which is treated with mebendazole or albendazole. Metronidazole treats protozoa such as Giardia and Entamoeba, not pinworm. Oral vancomycin treats C. difficile colitis, not helminths. Sources: Robbins Ch. 8 (Enterobiasis); Katzung Ch. 53 (Mebendazole). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: pinworm eggs are visualized via the tape test and treated with bendazoles or pyrantel pamoate (First Aid Microbiology (Nematodes)). COMLEX: pinworm eggs mature rapidly and become infectious in about 6 hours (Robbins Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 1 — pharmacology and clinical uses of antihelminthic drugs","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Niclosamide treats tapeworms, not pinworm.","C":"Ivermectin is the drug of choice for Strongyloides, not pinw","D":"Metronidazole treats protozoa such as Giardia and Entamoeba,","E":"Oral vancomycin treats C. difficile colitis, not helminths."}},{"id":"sdl35_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the diagnostic findings and appropriate pharmacologic treatments of major intestinal helminth infections, including identification of eggs, larvae, or adult worms and the mechanisms of action of commonly used anthelmintic agents.","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old man from rural Kentucky has 3 months of intermittent diarrhea and bloating. His eosinophils are 14% (reference range: 1–3%). Stool microscopy shows motile larvae; no ova are seen. Which mechanism explains the action of the first-line drug for this infection?","choices":{"A":"Blocked tubulin polymerization in cells","B":"Intensified GABA signaling in nerves","C":"Increased calcium influx in tegument","D":"Blocked folate synthesis in cytoplasm","E":"Inhibited cell wall synthesis in worms"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Larvae rather than ova in stool indicate Strongyloides stercoralis, whose larvae are mostly passed in the stool. Its drug of choice, ivermectin, paralyzes nematodes by intensifying GABA-mediated transmission in peripheral nerves. Blocking tubulin polymerization is the benzimidazole mechanism, not that of ivermectin, the Strongyloides drug of choice. Calcium influx in the tegument is praziquantel's action against flukes and tapeworms. Blocking folate synthesis is the sulfonamide action on bacterial dihydropteroate synthase, not the action of an antihelminthic. Cell wall synthesis inhibition targets bacterial peptidoglycan, a polymer unique to bacteria and absent from worms. Sources: Robbins Ch. 8 (Strongyloidiasis); Katzung Ch. 53 (Ivermectin; Table 53-1). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: rhabditiform larvae of Strongyloides are seen in feces under the microscope (First Aid Microbiology (Nematodes)). COMLEX: Strongyloides is endemic in tropical regions and occurs sporadically in Appalachia (Robbins Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 1 — pharmacology and clinical uses of antihelminthic drugs","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Blocking tubulin polymerization is the benzimidazole mechani","C":"Calcium influx in the tegument is praziquantel's action agai","D":"Blocking folate synthesis is the sulfonamide action on bacte","E":"Cell wall synthesis inhibition targets bacterial peptidoglyc"}},{"id":"sdl35_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the diagnostic findings and appropriate pharmacologic treatments of major intestinal helminth infections, including identification of eggs, larvae, or adult worms and the mechanisms of action of commonly used anthelmintic agents.","batch":2,"stem":"An 8-year-old boy who recently moved from rural Bangladesh passes a 20-cm pink worm from his rectum. Stool examination shows numerous eggs. His hemoglobin is 11.9 g/dL (reference range: 11.5–15.5 g/dL). Which drug is first-line for this infection?","choices":{"A":"Niclosamide","B":"Diethylcarbamazine","C":"Albendazole","D":"Doxycycline","E":"Metronidazole"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Adult worms 15 to 35 cm long passed from the rectum, with eggs in stool, diagnose Ascaris lumbricoides. Katzung lists albendazole, pyrantel pamoate, or mebendazole as the drugs of choice. Niclosamide treats tapeworms, not the roundworm Ascaris. Diethylcarbamazine treats filarial infections, not Ascaris. Metronidazole treats protozoa, not intestinal roundworms. Doxycycline acts indirectly by killing Wolbachia, a symbiont of filarial parasites, so it is not a treatment for an intestinal roundworm. Sources: Robbins Ch. 8 (Ascariasis); Katzung Ch. 53 (Table 53-1). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Ascaris has knobby-coated oval eggs in feces and is treated with bendazoles (First Aid Microbiology (Nematodes)). COMLEX: each adult female Ascaris releases about 200,000 eggs per day into the stool (Robbins Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 1 — pharmacology and clinical uses of antihelminthic drugs","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Niclosamide treats tapeworms, not the roundworm Ascaris.","B":"Diethylcarbamazine treats filarial infections, not Ascaris.","E":"Metronidazole treats protozoa, not intestinal roundworms.","D":"Doxycycline acts indirectly by killing Wolbachia, a symbiont"}},{"id":"trial_sdl35_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the modes of transmission and life cycle entry points of major intestinal helminths, including ingestion of eggs or encysted larvae, and skin penetration by larvae.","batch":3,"stem":"A 45-year-old man in northern Minnesota has megaloblastic anemia, and his stool shows broad tapeworm segments. He eats home-cured raw lake fish. How did he acquire the parasite?","choices":{"A":"Metacercariae on water plants","B":"Larvae in raw fish","C":"Bite of an infected sandfly","D":"Eggs shed by household dogs","E":"Cercariae in fresh lake water"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Diphyllobothrium latum, the fish tapeworm, is acquired by eating undercooked or raw fish and lives only in the gut. Sources: Robbins Ch. 8 (Cysticercosis and tapeworm infections). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the fish tapeworm competes for vitamin B12 in the intestine, causing megaloblastic anemia (First Aid Microbiology (Cestodes)). COMLEX: the fish tapeworm does not form cysticerci in humans (Robbins Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 4 — vitamin B12 absorption and the terminal ileum","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl35_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain how the worm burden in helminth infections is determined and differentiate species capable of autoinfection from those that are not.","batch":3,"stem":"A 6-year-old girl with pinworm infection scratches her perianal skin at night. About how soon do freshly laid eggs become infectious?","choices":{"A":"About 6 hours","B":"About 6 days","C":"About 2 weeks","D":"About 6 weeks","E":"About 6 months"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Enterobius eggs deposited on perianal skin mature rapidly and are infectious in approximately 6 hours, so scratching and hand-to-mouth spread allow quick reinfection. Sources: Robbins Ch. 8 (Enterobiasis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 35 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Enterobius causes anal pruritus that is worse at night (First Aid Microbiology (Nematodes)). COMLEX: the eggs spread from hands or bed linens (Robbins Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 5 — defecation reflex and the anal sphincters","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins"},{"id":"trial_sdl35_q03","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify clinical manifestations associated with specific intestinal helminths and describe mechanisms of disease including nutrient competition, mechanical obstruction, and mucosal damage.","batch":3,"stem":"A 5-year-old boy in rural Southeast Asia has a heavy whipworm (Trichuris trichiura) burden with loose stools and anemia. Which complication is classically described in children?","choices":{"A":"Biliary obstruction","B":"Hepatic cysts","C":"Rectal prolapse","D":"Seizure disorder","E":"Myocarditis"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Trichuris trichiura is often asymptomatic but can cause loose stools, anemia, and rectal prolapse in children. Biliary obstruction is a complication of Ascaris, which can form worm balls and migrate into the biliary tree. Sources: First Aid Microbiology (Nematodes) (Robbins Ch. 8 does not cover Trichuris; Elentra objective framing). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Trichuris is spread fecal-orally and treated with bendazoles (First Aid Microbiology (Nematodes)). COMLEX: Trichuris infection is often asymptomatic, so symptoms track heavy worm burden (First Aid Microbiology (Nematodes)).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 1 — gross and mucosal features of the large intestine","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Biliary obstruction is a complication of Ascaris, which can "}},{"id":"trial_sdl35_q04","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the role of eosinophilia in helminth infections and describe when eosinophilia is expected based on tissue invasion.","batch":3,"stem":"A duodenal biopsy from a 38-year-old woman with mild strongyloidiasis shows larvae in the crypts without tissue invasion. Which inflammatory pattern is expected in the lamina propria?","choices":{"A":"Eosinophil-rich infiltrate","B":"Neutrophilic crypt abscesses","C":"Noncaseating granulomas","D":"Dense plasma cell sheets","E":"Transmural lymphoid nodules"},"correct":"A","explanation":"In mild strongyloidiasis, larvae are in the duodenal crypts but not the underlying tissue, with an eosinophil-rich lamina propria infiltrate and mucosal edema. Neutrophilic crypt abscesses are characteristic of ulcerative colitis. Noncaseating granulomas are characteristic of Crohn disease. Sources: Robbins Ch. 8 (Strongyloidiasis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 35 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: eosinophils defend against helminths and release major basic protein (First Aid Hematology (Eosinophils)). COMLEX: in hyperinfection, many adult worms, larvae, and eggs fill the duodenal and ileal crypts with a mononuclear infiltrate (Robbins Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 4 — small-intestine epithelial renewal","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins","whyNot":{"B":"Neutrophilic crypt abscesses are characteristic of ulcerativ","C":"Noncaseating granulomas are characteristic of Crohn disease."}},{"id":"trial_sdl35_q05","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the diagnostic findings and appropriate pharmacologic treatments of major intestinal helminth infections, including identification of eggs, larvae, or adult worms and the mechanisms of action of commonly used anthelmintic agents.","batch":3,"stem":"A 61-year-old woman from eastern Tennessee has 4 months of intermittent loose stools and bloating. Her eosinophils are 11% (reference range: 1–3%). On a stool ova and parasite examination, which parasite form is most typical of the likely nematode?","choices":{"A":"Plugged barrel eggs","B":"Rhabditoid larvae","C":"Operculated eggs","D":"Mammillated eggs","E":"Gravid proglottids"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Strongyloides stercoralis produces eggs in the intestinal mucosa, but most larvae hatch and are passed in the stool, so stool examination shows rhabditoid larvae. Mammillated eggs are the stool finding in Ascaris infection. Gravid proglottids in stool indicate a tapeworm, not a nematode. Sources: Robbins Ch. 8 (Strongyloidiasis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Strongyloides causes duodenitis, dry cough, and pruritus, and hyperinfection occurs with immunosuppression (First Aid Microbiology (Nematodes)). COMLEX: Strongyloides females reproduce by parthenogenesis in the small-intestinal mucosa (Robbins Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 1 — pharmacology and clinical uses of antihelminthic drugs","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"Mammillated eggs are the stool finding in Ascaris infection.","E":"Gravid proglottids in stool indicate a tapeworm, not a nemat"}},{"id":"trial_sdl35_q06","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify clinical manifestations associated with specific intestinal helminths and describe mechanisms of disease including nutrient competition, mechanical obstruction, and mucosal damage.","batch":3,"stem":"A 29-year-old man has had cystic brain lesions without symptoms for years. He now has new seizures, and imaging shows edema surrounding one lesion. What best explains the change?","choices":{"A":"Adult worm growth in the brain","B":"Cyst rupture into the bloodstream","C":"New egg release within the cyst","D":"Inflammation around a dying cyst","E":"Bacterial abscess within the cyst"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Viable Taenia solium cysts often do not cause symptoms and evade immunity, but an inflammatory response develops when cysticerci die and degenerate. Sources: Robbins Ch. 8 (Cysticercosis and tapeworm infections). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: neurocysticercosis is treated with albendazole (First Aid Microbiology (Cestodes)). COMLEX: larval cysts in tissues outside the intestine do not develop into mature worms (Robbins Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 2 — choosing among antihelminthic drugs","isHighYield":true},{"id":"grid_sdl35_q01","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain how the worm burden in helminth infections is determined and differentiate species capable of autoinfection from those that are not.","batch":4,"stem":"A 58-year-old man who emigrated from rural Southeast Asia 30 years ago is treated with high-dose prednisone for a flare of chronic obstructive pulmonary disease. Two weeks later he has abdominal pain, diarrhea, cough, and wheezing, followed by fever and hypotension; blood cultures grow Escherichia coli. Microscopy of stool and sputum shows motile larvae. Which of the following sets of features best characterizes the organism causing this patient's illness?","grid":{"headers":["Entry route","Autoinfection","Eosinophilia"]},"choices":{"A":["Egg ingestion","No","Present"],"B":["Skin penetration","No","Present"],"C":["Skin penetration","Yes","Present"],"D":["Egg ingestion","Yes","Absent"],"E":["Larvae in fish","No","Absent"]},"correct":"C","explanation":"Choice C (skin penetration, autoinfection yes, eosinophilia present) is correct. Strongyloides stercoralis filariform larvae in soil penetrate intact skin and migrate through the lungs to the gut. The notes describe rhabditiform larvae that molt into filariform larvae inside the host and re-penetrate the bowel wall or perianal skin, so infection can persist for decades and become hyperinfection with gram-negative sepsis after corticosteroids; tissue migration causes eosinophilia, though steroids can blunt it. Choice A (egg ingestion, no autoinfection, eosinophilia present) is Ascaris lumbricoides, whose eggs hatch in the small intestine and whose larvae migrate through the lungs, causing transient eosinophilic pneumonitis, but which needs soil maturation and cannot reinfect the host. Choice B (skin penetration, no autoinfection, eosinophilia present) is hookworm (Necator, Ancylostoma), which enters through skin and migrates through the lungs but requires external development. Choice D (egg ingestion, autoinfection yes, eosinophilia absent) is Enterobius vermicularis, which reinfects by hand-to-mouth transfer of perianal eggs but stays in the lumen. Choice E (larvae in fish, no autoinfection, eosinophilia absent) is Diphyllobothrium latum, acquired from undercooked freshwater fish and confined to the lumen, where it competes for vitamin B12. Sources: Exam 3 lecture notes (SDL 35, sections 1, 2, and 4: transmission, autoinfection, and eosinophilia); Robbins Ch. 8 (helminths). Suppression of eosinophilia by corticosteroids is from general clinical knowledge and is not in the notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Strongyloides is the intestinal nematode that autoinfects, persists for decades, and causes hyperinfection with gram-negative sepsis on corticosteroids; stool shows larvae, not eggs, and ivermectin is the drug of choice (Exam 3 notes, SDL 35). COMLEX: screen and treat Strongyloides before steroids in patients from endemic areas.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 36 Obj 2 — choosing ivermectin versus albendazole versus praziquantel","isHighYield":true}],"36":[{"id":"sdl36_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the basic pharmacology, clinical uses and adverse reactions for the following medications used in the treatment of helminthic infections: albendazole, bithionol, diethylcarbamazine, doxycycline, ivermectin, mebendazole, moxidectin, praziquantal and thiabendazole.","batch":1,"stem":"Benzimidazoles such as mebendazole are thought to kill nematodes by inhibiting what?","choices":{"A":"Ergosterol synthesis","B":"Folate synthesis","C":"Peptidoglycan synthesis","D":"Chitin synthesis","E":"Microtubule synthesis"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Benzimidazoles, including mebendazole and albendazole, are thought to act against nematodes by inhibiting microtubule synthesis. Ergosterol is the fungal membrane sterol targeted by antifungals such as amphotericin B, not by benzimidazoles. Folate synthesis via dihydropteroate synthase is the target of sulfonamides, not of benzimidazoles. Peptidoglycan is the bacterial cell wall polymer whose synthesis beta-lactam antibiotics block; it is not a target of antihelminthics. Sources: Katzung Ch. 53 (Albendazole; Mebendazole). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: mebendazole binds tubulin and is listed with the microtubule-acting drugs (First Aid Microbiology (Antiparasitic drugs / helminth tables)). COMLEX: less than 10% of an oral mebendazole dose is absorbed (Katzung Ch. 53).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 5 — drug and toxin induced liver injury","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Ergosterol is the fungal membrane sterol targeted by antifun","B":"Folate synthesis via dihydropteroate synthase is the target ","C":"Peptidoglycan is the bacterial cell wall polymer whose synth"}},{"id":"sdl36_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the basic pharmacology, clinical uses and adverse reactions for the following medications used in the treatment of helminthic infections: albendazole, bithionol, diethylcarbamazine, doxycycline, ivermectin, mebendazole, moxidectin, praziquantal and thiabendazole.","batch":1,"stem":"Which antibiotic kills adult filarial worms indirectly by eliminating their Wolbachia bacterial symbionts?","choices":{"A":"Doxycycline","B":"Azithromycin","C":"Ciprofloxacin","D":"Vancomycin","E":"Clindamycin"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Doxycycline has macrofilaricidal activity against Wuchereria bancrofti and activity against onchocerciasis because it kills Wolbachia, an intracellular bacterial symbiont of filarial parasites. Azithromycin is a macrolide that binds the 50S ribosomal subunit; it is not the drug used to clear Wolbachia from filarial worms. Ciprofloxacin is a quinolone that inhibits DNA gyrase and topoisomerase IV; it is not the drug used to clear Wolbachia from filarial worms. Vancomycin binds D-Ala-D-Ala to block cell wall synthesis in gram-positive bacteria; it is not the drug used to clear Wolbachia. Clindamycin is a lincosamide that shares the 50S ribosomal binding site with macrolides; it is not the drug used to clear Wolbachia. Sources: Katzung Ch. 53 (Doxycycline). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: tetracyclines cause tooth discoloration and are generally avoided in pregnancy and in children, except doxycycline (First Aid Antimicrobials — Tetracyclines). COMLEX: Katzung reports better activity against adult W bancrofti than any other available drug (Katzung Ch. 53).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 5 — drug and toxin induced liver injury","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Azithromycin is a macrolide that binds the 50S ribosomal sub","C":"Ciprofloxacin is a quinolone that inhibits DNA gyrase and to","D":"Vancomycin binds D-Ala-D-Ala to block cell wall synthesis in","E":"Clindamycin is a lincosamide that shares the 50S ribosomal b"}},{"id":"sdl36_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the basic pharmacology, clinical uses and adverse reactions for the following medications used in the treatment of helminthic infections: albendazole, bithionol, diethylcarbamazine, doxycycline, ivermectin, mebendazole, moxidectin, praziquantal and thiabendazole.","batch":1,"stem":"Praziquantel paralyzes trematodes and cestodes by increasing their membrane permeability to which ion?","choices":{"A":"Chloride","B":"Sodium","C":"Calcium","D":"Potassium","E":"Magnesium"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Praziquantel increases the permeability of trematode and cestode cell membranes to calcium, causing paralysis, dislodgement, and death of the worm. Increased chloride signaling via GABA or glutamate-gated channels is ivermectin's mechanism, not praziquantel's. Praziquantel increases the permeability of trematode and cestode cell membranes to calcium, not sodium, causing paralysis and dislodgement. Praziquantel increases trematode and cestode membrane permeability to calcium, not potassium. Praziquantel increases trematode and cestode membrane permeability to calcium, not magnesium. Sources: Katzung Ch. 53 (Praziquantel). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: praziquantel treats Schistosoma, Clonorchis, and Taenia infections (First Aid Microbiology (Antiparasitic drugs / helminth tables)). COMLEX: praziquantel tablets are swallowed without chewing because the bitter taste can induce retching (Katzung Ch. 53).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 5 — drug and toxin induced liver injury","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Increased chloride signaling via GABA or glutamate-gated cha","B":"Praziquantel increases the permeability of trematode and cestode cell membranes to calcium, n","D":"Praziquantel increases trematode and cestode membrane permea","E":"Praziquantel increases trematode and cestode membrane permeability to calcium, not m"}},{"id":"sdl36_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Appreciate when one antihelminthic medication may be chosen over another in a clinical scenario.","batch":1,"stem":"Which drug of choice is used for lymphatic filariasis from Wuchereria bancrofti and for loiasis?","choices":{"A":"Diethylcarbamazine","B":"Triclabendazole","C":"Pyrantel pamoate","D":"Piperazine citrate","E":"Niclosamide tablets"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Diethylcarbamazine is a drug of choice for W bancrofti, Brugia, and Loa loa because of its efficacy and lack of serious toxicity; it immobilizes microfilariae and exposes them to host defenses. Triclabendazole is the drug of choice for Fasciola hepatica, not filariasis. Pyrantel pamoate treats luminal nematodes such as Ascaris and pinworm, not filariae. Niclosamide is a second-line drug for tapeworm infections and has no role in filariasis or loiasis. Piperazine is an alternative drug for ascariasis only, not a treatment for filariasis or loiasis. Sources: Katzung Ch. 53 (Diethylcarbamazine citrate). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Loa loa (deer fly) and Wuchereria bancrofti (mosquito) are both treated with diethylcarbamazine (First Aid Microbiology (Antiparasitic drugs / helminth tables)). COMLEX: the plasma half-life of diethylcarbamazine is longer in alkaline urine (Katzung Ch. 53).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 → Obj 3 — clinical manifestations of intestinal helminths","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Triclabendazole is the drug of choice for Fasciola hepatica,","C":"Pyrantel pamoate treats luminal nematodes such as Ascaris an","E":"Niclosamide is a second-line drug for tapeworm infections an","D":"Piperazine is an alternative drug for ascariasis only, not a"}},{"id":"sdl36_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Appreciate when one antihelminthic medication may be chosen over another in a clinical scenario.","batch":1,"stem":"For neurocysticercosis, why is albendazole now generally preferred over praziquantel?","choices":{"A":"Avoids need for corticosteroids","B":"Single oral dose regimen","C":"No liver toxicity with long use","D":"Better subarachnoid penetration","E":"Proven safety in early pregnancy"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Albendazole is preferred for neurocysticercosis because of a shorter course, lower cost, improved penetration into the subarachnoid space, and higher drug levels with corticosteroids. Albendazole does not avoid corticosteroids; its levels actually rise when steroids are co-administered. Albendazole is preferred for a shorter course, not a single-dose regimen. Long-term albendazole can cause hepatotoxicity and requires liver monitoring. Albendazole is contraindicated in pregnancy. Sources: Katzung Ch. 53 (Albendazole). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: neurocysticercosis is treated with albendazole, while intestinal taeniasis is treated with praziquantel (First Aid Microbiology (Antiparasitic drugs / helminth tables)). COMLEX: corticosteroids are usually given with the antihelminthic to limit inflammation from dying organisms (Katzung Ch. 53).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 → Obj 3 — clinical manifestations of intestinal helminths","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Albendazole does not avoid corticosteroids; its levels actua","B":"Albendazole is preferred for a shorter course, not a single-","C":"Long-term albendazole can cause hepatotoxicity and requires ","E":"Albendazole is contraindicated in pregnancy."}},{"id":"sdl36_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Appreciate when one antihelminthic medication may be chosen over another in a clinical scenario.","batch":1,"stem":"For uncomplicated strongyloidiasis, which drug is given as 200 mcg/kg once daily for 2 days?","choices":{"A":"Praziquantel","B":"Ivermectin","C":"Niclosamide","D":"Oxamniquine","E":"Pyrantel pamoate"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Ivermectin is the drug of choice for strongyloidiasis, given as 200 mcg/kg once daily for 2 days; thiabendazole is an alternative but is more toxic. Praziquantel treats flukes and tapeworms, not Strongyloides. Pyrantel pamoate is for Ascaris and pinworm, whereas ivermectin is the drug of choice for strongyloidiasis. Niclosamide is a second-line drug for tapeworms, whereas ivermectin is the drug of choice for strongyloidiasis. Oxamniquine is an alternative to praziquantel for Schistosoma mansoni, not a treatment for strongyloidiasis. Sources: Katzung Ch. 53 (Ivermectin; Thiabendazole). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Strongyloides is treated with ivermectin or bendazoles (First Aid Microbiology (Antiparasitic drugs / helminth tables)). COMLEX: in disseminated disease in immunosuppressed patients, monthly suppressive therapy may help (Katzung Ch. 53).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 → Obj 3 — clinical manifestations of intestinal helminths","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Praziquantel treats flukes and tapeworms, not Strongyloides.","E":"Pyrantel pamoate is for Ascaris and pinworm, whereas ivermec","C":"Niclosamide is a second-line drug for tapeworms, whereas ive","D":"Oxamniquine is an alternative to praziquantel for Schistosom"}},{"id":"sdl36_q07","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the basic pharmacology, clinical uses and adverse reactions for the following medications used in the treatment of helminthic infections: albendazole, bithionol, diethylcarbamazine, doxycycline, ivermectin, mebendazole, moxidectin, praziquantal and thiabendazole.","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old sheep farmer with a large hepatic hydatid cyst starts several months of albendazole before surgery. Which monitoring is advised during this long course?","choices":{"A":"Blood counts and liver tests","B":"Serum creatinine and urinalysis","C":"Serial ECG for QT changes","D":"Audiometry and visual fields","E":"Serum magnesium and phosphate"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Long-term albendazole for hydatid disease is usually well tolerated but can cause raised liver enzymes and pancytopenia, so blood counts and liver function are monitored. Long-term albendazole threatens the liver and marrow, not the kidneys, so creatinine and urinalysis are not the advised monitoring. Long-term albendazole calls for monitoring of blood counts and liver function; serial ECGs for QT change are not the advised monitoring. Long-term albendazole calls for monitoring of blood counts and liver function, not serum magnesium and phosphate. Sources: Katzung Ch. 53 (Albendazole). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Echinococcus hydatid cysts are treated with albendazole, with surgery for some cysts (First Aid Microbiology (Antiparasitic drugs / helminth tables)). COMLEX: albendazole should be avoided in patients with cirrhosis (Katzung Ch. 53).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 5 — drug and toxin induced liver injury","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Long-term albendazole threatens the liver and marrow, not th","C":"Long-term albendazole calls for monitoring of blood counts a","E":"Long-term albendazole calls for monitoring of blood counts and liver function,"}},{"id":"sdl36_q08","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the basic pharmacology, clinical uses and adverse reactions for the following medications used in the treatment of helminthic infections: albendazole, bithionol, diethylcarbamazine, doxycycline, ivermectin, mebendazole, moxidectin, praziquantal and thiabendazole.","batch":2,"stem":"A 26-year-old woman at 8 weeks' gestation has perianal itching, and a tape test shows pinworm eggs. Her physician defers mebendazole. What property of mebendazole explains this?","choices":{"A":"Causes neonatal gray syndrome","B":"Inhibits fetal folate uptake","C":"Causes fetal tooth staining","D":"Teratogenic in animal studies","E":"Causes fetal renal tubule damage"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Mebendazole is teratogenic in animals and is therefore contraindicated in pregnancy. Fetal tooth staining is a doxycycline (tetracycline) concern, not a mebendazole one. Neonatal gray syndrome results from chloramphenicol accumulation in infants, not from mebendazole. Mebendazole is deferred in pregnancy because it is teratogenic in animals, not because it blocks fetal folate uptake. Mebendazole is contraindicated in pregnancy because it is teratogenic in animals, not because of fetal renal tubule damage. Sources: Katzung Ch. 53 (Mebendazole). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Katzung) — detail not in SDL 36 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Enterobius is treated with bendazoles or pyrantel (First Aid Microbiology (Antiparasitic drugs / helminth tables)). COMLEX: for pinworm, mebendazole 100 mg is given once and repeated at 2 weeks (Katzung Ch. 53).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 → Obj 1 — transmission and entry routes of intestinal helminths","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Katzung","whyNot":{"C":"Fetal tooth staining is a doxycycline (tetracycline) concern","A":"Neonatal gray syndrome results from chloramphenicol accumula","B":"Mebendazole is deferred in pregnancy because it is teratogen","E":"Mebendazole is contraindicated in pregnancy because it is te"}},{"id":"sdl36_q09","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the basic pharmacology, clinical uses and adverse reactions for the following medications used in the treatment of helminthic infections: albendazole, bithionol, diethylcarbamazine, doxycycline, ivermectin, mebendazole, moxidectin, praziquantal and thiabendazole.","batch":2,"stem":"A 40-year-old man from Ghana with onchocerciasis receives a single oral dose of the drug of choice, a macrocyclic lactone. Concurrent use of which drug class should be avoided?","choices":{"A":"Proton pump inhibitors","B":"Thiazide diuretics","C":"Beta-blockers","D":"ACE inhibitors","E":"Benzodiazepines"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Ivermectin paralyzes nematodes by intensifying GABA-mediated transmission, so it is best not combined with other GABA-enhancing drugs such as barbiturates, benzodiazepines, or valproic acid. Ivermectin intensifies GABA-mediated transmission, so the drugs to avoid are other GABA enhancers (barbiturates, benzodiazepines, valproic acid), not proton pump inhibitors. Ivermectin intensifies GABA-mediated transmission, so the drugs to avoid are other GABA enhancers (barbiturates, benzodiazepines, valproic acid), not thiazide diuretics. Ivermectin intensifies GABA-mediated transmission, so the drugs to avoid are other GABA enhancers (barbiturates, benzodiazepines, valproic acid), not beta-blockers. Sources: Katzung Ch. 53 (Ivermectin). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Onchocerca volvulus causes black skin nodules and river blindness (First Aid Microbiology (Antiparasitic drugs / helminth tables)). COMLEX: treatment reactions in onchocerciasis come mainly from killing microfilariae and peak on day 2 (Katzung Ch. 53).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 → Obj 4 — eosinophilia and tissue invasion in helminth infections","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Ivermectin intensifies GABA-mediated transmission, so the dr","B":"Ivermectin intensifies GABA-mediated transmission, so the drugs to avoid are other GABA enhancers (barbiturates, benzodiazepines, valproic acid), not t","C":"Ivermectin intensifies GABA-mediated transmission, so the drugs to avoid are other GABA enhancers (barbiturates, benzodiazepines, valproic acid), not b"}},{"id":"sdl36_q10","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Appreciate when one antihelminthic medication may be chosen over another in a clinical scenario.","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old man from Cameroon has a worm crossing his conjunctiva and a very high Loa loa microfilarial load, well above the 2500/mL high-risk level. Which initial approach is safest?","choices":{"A":"Immediate full-dose diethylcarbamazine","B":"Single-dose praziquantel","C":"Slower-acting albendazole first","D":"Topical thiabendazole cream","E":"Weekly oral niclosamide"},"correct":"C","explanation":"For heavy Loa loa burdens (more than 2500 parasites/mL), Katzung lists apheresis or therapy with albendazole, which is slower acting and better tolerated, before definitive filaricidal treatment. Full-dose DEC in heavy Loa loa loads risks encephalopathy and retinal hemorrhage, so burden is lowered first. Praziquantel treats flukes and tapeworms and has no role in loiasis. Thiabendazole is an alternative for strongyloidiasis and larva migrans, not loiasis. Niclosamide is a second-line tapeworm drug, minimally absorbed from the gut, with no activity against Loa loa microfilariae. Sources: Katzung Ch. 53 (Diethylcarbamazine citrate; Ivermectin). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Loa loa causes skin swelling and a worm in the conjunctiva (First Aid Microbiology (Antiparasitic drugs / helminth tables)). COMLEX: reactions to dying microfilariae are occasionally severe in L loa infections (Katzung Ch. 53).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 → Obj 4 — eosinophilia and tissue invasion in helminth infections","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Full-dose DEC in heavy Loa loa loads risks encephalopathy an","B":"Praziquantel treats flukes and tapeworms and has no role in ","D":"Thiabendazole is an alternative for strongyloidiasis and lar","E":"Niclosamide is a second-line tapeworm drug, minimally absorb"}},{"id":"sdl36_q11","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Appreciate when one antihelminthic medication may be chosen over another in a clinical scenario.","batch":2,"stem":"A 22-year-old woman with symptomatic parenchymal neurocysticercosis is taking dexamethasone for cerebral edema. What effect does dexamethasone have on praziquantel?","choices":{"A":"Reduced plasma drug levels","B":"Increased plasma drug levels","C":"Prolonged elimination half-life","D":"Greater CSF drug penetration","E":"Faster cyst calcification"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Corticosteroids can decrease plasma praziquantel levels by up to 50%, whereas albendazole levels increase, one reason albendazole is preferred when steroids are needed. Dexamethasone lowers praziquantel levels by up to 50%; it is albendazole levels that rise. Superior CSF penetration is an albendazole advantage, not an effect of dexamethasone on praziquantel. Corticosteroids lower the plasma level of praziquantel by up to 50% rather than prolonging its half-life. Dexamethasone is given with praziquantel to blunt the inflammatory response to dying cysts, and it lowers praziquantel plasma levels; it does not speed cyst calcification. Sources: Katzung Ch. 53 (Praziquantel; Albendazole). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Taenia solium cysticercosis follows ingestion of eggs in fecally contaminated food (First Aid Microbiology (Antiparasitic drugs / helminth tables)). COMLEX: some workers caution against praziquantel in spinal neurocysticercosis (Katzung Ch. 53).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 → Obj 3 — clinical manifestations of intestinal helminths","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Dexamethasone lowers praziquantel levels by up to 50%; it is","D":"Superior CSF penetration is an albendazole advantage, not an","C":"Corticosteroids lower the plasma level of praziquantel by up","E":"Dexamethasone is given with praziquantel to blunt the inflam"}},{"id":"sdl36_q12","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Appreciate when one antihelminthic medication may be chosen over another in a clinical scenario.","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old man from Peru has right upper quadrant pain, eosinophilia, and Fasciola hepatica eggs after eating watercress. Why is praziquantel a poor choice for him?","choices":{"A":"Excessive toxicity in hepatic disease","B":"Lack of activity in this infection","C":"Poor oral bioavailability in adults","D":"Need for prolonged IV dosing","E":"High rates of liver fluke relapse"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Praziquantel treats most trematodes but was not effective for fascioliasis even at high doses; triclabendazole is the drug of choice, with bithionol as an alternative. Praziquantel has about 80% oral bioavailability; its problem in fascioliasis is Fasciola resistance. Praziquantel is given orally; it fails in fascioliasis because Fasciola is resistant. Praziquantel is generally well tolerated even in the hepatosplenic stage of advanced schistosomiasis; its problem in fascioliasis is lack of efficacy. Sources: Katzung Ch. 53 (Praziquantel; Triclabendazole; Bithionol). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: praziquantel treats trematodes such as Schistosoma and Clonorchis (First Aid Microbiology (Antiparasitic drugs / helminth tables)). COMLEX: triclabendazole may prolong the QT interval (Katzung Ch. 53).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 3 — hydatid cysts and other parasitic liver infections","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Praziquantel has about 80% oral bioavailability; its problem","D":"Praziquantel is given orally; it fails in fascioliasis becau","A":"Praziquantel is generally well tolerated even in the hepatos"}},{"id":"trial_sdl36_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the basic pharmacology, clinical uses and adverse reactions for the following medications used in the treatment of helminthic infections: albendazole, bithionol, diethylcarbamazine, doxycycline, ivermectin, mebendazole, moxidectin, praziquantal and thiabendazole.","batch":3,"stem":"A 60-year-old man with hydatid liver disease starts albendazole. How should he take it for this tissue infection?","choices":{"A":"With a fatty meal","B":"On an empty stomach","C":"With an antacid dose","D":"With grapefruit juice","E":"At bedtime with milk"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Albendazole is taken on an empty stomach for intraluminal parasites but with a fatty meal for tissue parasites, which increases its erratic absorption. Sources: Katzung Ch. 53 (Albendazole). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: albendazole is used for Echinococcus and neurocysticercosis (First Aid Microbiology (Antiparasitic drugs / helminth tables)). COMLEX: albendazole is converted on first pass to the active metabolite albendazole sulfoxide (Katzung Ch. 53).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 3 — hydatid cysts and other parasitic liver infections","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl36_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the basic pharmacology, clinical uses and adverse reactions for the following medications used in the treatment of helminthic infections: albendazole, bithionol, diethylcarbamazine, doxycycline, ivermectin, mebendazole, moxidectin, praziquantal and thiabendazole.","batch":3,"stem":"A 33-year-old woman has strongyloidiasis. Why has thiabendazole been largely replaced by other agents?","choices":{"A":"Lower cure rates than other agents","B":"Greater toxicity than other agents","C":"Need for prolonged IV infusions","D":"Widespread parasite drug resistance","E":"Poor absorption from the gut"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Thiabendazole is much more toxic than other benzimidazoles and ivermectin; irreversible liver failure and fatal Stevens-Johnson syndrome have been reported. Sources: Katzung Ch. 53 (Thiabendazole). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: bendazoles treat most intestinal nematodes (First Aid Microbiology (Antiparasitic drugs / helminth tables)). COMLEX: thiabendazole cream can be applied topically for cutaneous larva migrans (Katzung Ch. 53).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 → Obj 2 — worm burden and autoinfection","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl36_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the basic pharmacology, clinical uses and adverse reactions for the following medications used in the treatment of helminthic infections: albendazole, bithionol, diethylcarbamazine, doxycycline, ivermectin, mebendazole, moxidectin, praziquantal and thiabendazole.","batch":3,"stem":"A 30-year-old man with river blindness receives a single 8-mg oral dose of a drug approved in 2018 with a half-life of 3–4 weeks. Which drug is it?","choices":{"A":"Triclabendazole","B":"Oxamniquine","C":"Moxidectin","D":"Niclosamide","E":"Metrifonate"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Moxidectin, a macrocyclic lactone approved in 2018 for onchocerciasis in persons 12 years or older, is given once orally and has a half-life of 3–4 weeks. Triclabendazole is the drug of choice for fascioliasis, not onchocerciasis. Sources: Katzung Ch. 53 (Moxidectin). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Onchocerca volvulus is spread by the female black fly (First Aid Microbiology (Antiparasitic drugs / helminth tables)). COMLEX: moxidectin lowered skin microfilarial loads more than the older standard drug in a recent trial (Katzung Ch. 53).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 → Obj 4 — eosinophilia and tissue invasion in helminth infections","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Triclabendazole is the drug of choice for fascioliasis, not "}},{"id":"trial_sdl36_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Appreciate when one antihelminthic medication may be chosen over another in a clinical scenario.","batch":3,"stem":"A 41-year-old woman with fascioliasis has a long QT interval, so the drug of choice is withheld. Which alternative is listed for this infection?","choices":{"A":"Bithionol","B":"Praziquantel","C":"Pyrantel pamoate","D":"Mebendazole","E":"Oxamniquine"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Bithionol is an alternative to triclabendazole for fascioliasis and to praziquantel for paragonimiasis. Fasciola is resistant to praziquantel, so it is not an alternative for fascioliasis. Pyrantel pamoate is used for pinworm, Ascaris, and hookworm, not liver flukes. Mebendazole is used for intestinal roundworms, not fascioliasis. Sources: Katzung Ch. 53 (Bithionol; Triclabendazole). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Katzung) — detail not in SDL 36 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: liver flukes such as Clonorchis are acquired from undercooked fish (First Aid Microbiology (Antiparasitic drugs / helminth tables)). COMLEX: skin rashes after a week of bithionol suggest a reaction to antigens from dying worms (Katzung Ch. 53).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 3 — hydatid cysts and other parasitic liver infections","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Katzung","whyNot":{"B":"Fasciola is resistant to praziquantel, so it is not an alter","C":"Pyrantel pamoate is used for pinworm, Ascaris, and hookworm,","D":"Mebendazole is used for intestinal roundworms, not fasciolia"}},{"id":"trial_sdl36_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Appreciate when one antihelminthic medication may be chosen over another in a clinical scenario.","batch":3,"stem":"An 8-year-old boy with hookworm anemia is given albendazole rather than mebendazole. What is the main reason?","choices":{"A":"Lower cost per complete course of care","B":"Safety in the first trimester","C":"Once-weekly dosing schedule","D":"Better efficacy in this infection","E":"Topical form for skin lesions"},"correct":"D","explanation":"For hookworm infection and trichuriasis, albendazole 400 mg daily for 3 days is recommended, with improved efficacy over mebendazole for hookworm. Albendazole is contraindicated in pregnancy, so first-trimester safety is not an advantage. Sources: Katzung Ch. 53 (Albendazole). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Katzung) — detail not in SDL 36 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Ancylostoma and Necator cause microcytic anemia and are treated with bendazoles or pyrantel (First Aid Microbiology (Antiparasitic drugs / helminth tables)). COMLEX: mebendazole cure rates for hookworm are low, though worm burden falls sharply (Katzung Ch. 53).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 → Obj 3 — clinical manifestations of intestinal helminths","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Katzung","whyNot":{"B":"Albendazole is contraindicated in pregnancy, so first-trimes"}},{"id":"trial_sdl36_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Appreciate when one antihelminthic medication may be chosen over another in a clinical scenario.","batch":3,"stem":"A 30-year-old woman has many viable parenchymal brain cysts from Taenia solium. Which regimen improves the parasiticidal effect?","choices":{"A":"Niclosamide plus mebendazole","B":"Pyrantel plus piperazine","C":"Thiabendazole plus pyrantel","D":"Metrifonate plus oxamniquine","E":"Albendazole plus praziquantel"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Combining albendazole with praziquantel improves efficacy in patients with multiple brain cysts. Sources: Katzung Ch. 53 (Albendazole; Praziquantel). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Katzung) — detail not in SDL 36 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: cysticercosis is treated with albendazole, plus praziquantel in some cases (First Aid Microbiology (Antiparasitic drugs / helminth tables)). COMLEX: praziquantel is contraindicated in ocular cysticercosis (Katzung Ch. 53).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 → Obj 1 — transmission and entry routes of intestinal helminths","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Katzung"},{"id":"grid_sdl36_q01","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Appreciate when one antihelminthic medication may be chosen over another in a clinical scenario.","batch":4,"stem":"A 27-year-old man who emigrated from a rural area of Central America has a first generalized seizure. Brain CT shows multiple small cystic lesions, several containing an eccentric scolex. An ophthalmologic examination shows no ocular cysts. Dexamethasone is started, and an antiparasitic drug is chosen that gives a shorter course, reaches the subarachnoid space well, and is not weakened by the corticosteroid. Which of the following sets of features best characterizes the antiparasitic drug chosen?","grid":{"headers":["Molecular action","Level on steroids","Site of action"]},"choices":{"A":["↑ Ca²⁺ permeability","↓","Tissue and lumen"],"B":["Binds β-tubulin","↑","Tissue and lumen"],"C":["Binds β-tubulin","↔","Gut lumen"],"D":["Opens GABA/glutamate Cl⁻","↔","Tissue and lumen"],"E":["Binds β-tubulin","↑","Gut lumen"]},"correct":"B","explanation":"Choice B (binds β-tubulin, level rises on steroids, tissue and lumen) is correct. Albendazole is preferred over praziquantel for neurocysticercosis. The notes describe a benzimidazole that binds parasite β-tubulin to block microtubule synthesis and is converted to albendazole sulfoxide, which distributes widely into tissues, CSF, bile, and hydatid cysts. The course is shorter and cheaper, CSF penetration is better, and levels of the active metabolite rise when corticosteroids are added. Choice A (raises Ca²⁺ permeability, level falls on steroids, tissue and lumen) is praziquantel, which causes Ca²⁺ influx and paralysis of cestodes and trematodes, but dexamethasone lowers its plasma level by up to 50%. Choice C (binds β-tubulin, no steroid effect, gut lumen) is mebendazole, a benzimidazole with less than 10% systemic bioavailability that acts in the gut lumen against intestinal roundworms. Choice D (opens GABA/glutamate Cl⁻ channels, no steroid effect, tissue and lumen) is ivermectin, which paralyzes nematodes and arthropods and is the drug of choice for strongyloidiasis and onchocerciasis, not cysticercosis. Choice E (binds β-tubulin, level rises on steroids, gut lumen) matches no agent in the notes; it pairs the corticosteroid interaction of albendazole with the luminal action of mebendazole, but a luminal drug could not treat brain cysts. Sources: Exam 3 lecture notes (SDL 36, sections 1 and 2: albendazole, mebendazole, ivermectin, praziquantel, and drug choice in neurocysticercosis); Katzung Ch. 53 (antihelminthic drugs). The unchanged corticosteroid effect on mebendazole and ivermectin levels is inferred because the notes describe no such interaction.","boardPrep":"Step 1: neurocysticercosis → albendazole (β-tubulin) plus corticosteroids; steroids raise albendazole sulfoxide but lower praziquantel levels by up to 50% (Exam 3 notes, SDL 36). COMLEX: exclude ocular cysticercosis before therapy, because dying cysts in the eye cause irreversible damage.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 35 Obj 2 — Taenia solium cysticercosis from egg ingestion","isHighYield":true}],"37":[{"id":"sdl37_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the mechanisms of action, clinical uses, and important adverse effects of medications (prokinetic and cholinomimetic agents) used to stimulate gastrointestinal motility.","batch":1,"stem":"Metoclopramide speeds gastric emptying and raises lower esophageal sphincter pressure mainly by what action?","choices":{"A":"Muscarinic M3 receptor agonism","B":"Dopamine D2 receptor blockade","C":"Serotonin 5-HT3 receptor blockade","D":"Histamine H2 receptor blockade","E":"Mu opioid receptor agonism"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Metoclopramide is a D2 antagonist; blocking inhibitory dopamine receptors in the gut raises LES pressure, enhances esophageal peristalsis and speeds gastric emptying, with little effect on small bowel or colon. Direct muscarinic agonism is bethanechol's mechanism, not metoclopramide's. 5-HT3 blockade is alosetron's mechanism for IBS-D, not metoclopramide's prokinetic effect. Mu opioid agonism, as with loperamide, slows transit rather than speeding gastric emptying. H2 receptor blockade competitively inhibits parietal cell acid secretion and does not speed gastric emptying. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Dopamine Receptor Antagonists). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: metoclopramide is a D2 antagonist used for diabetic gastroparesis and persistent GERD (First Aid Gastrointestinal Pharmacology). COMLEX: metoclopramide also blocks D2 receptors in the area postrema chemoreceptor trigger zone (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 4 — CCK and GIP slow gastric emptying","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Direct muscarinic agonism is bethanechol's mechanism, not me","C":"5-HT3 blockade is alosetron's mechanism for IBS-D, not metoc","E":"Mu opioid agonism, as with loperamide, slows transit rather ","D":"H2 receptor blockade competitively inhibits parietal cell ac"}},{"id":"sdl37_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the mechanisms of action, clinical uses, and important adverse effects of medications (prokinetic and cholinomimetic agents) used to stimulate gastrointestinal motility.","batch":1,"stem":"Which agent given intravenously is used to treat acute colonic pseudo-obstruction (Ogilvie syndrome)?","choices":{"A":"Atropine","B":"Octreotide","C":"Ondansetron","D":"Neostigmine","E":"Dicyclomine"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Neostigmine, an acetylcholinesterase inhibitor, raises acetylcholine at enteric synapses; a 2 mg IV dose rapidly evacuates colonic gas and stool in acute pseudo-obstruction. Dicyclomine is an antispasmodic for IBS cramping and would worsen colonic pseudo-obstruction. The anticholinergic properties of atropine add to its antidiarrheal effect, the opposite of what colonic pseudo-obstruction needs; the acetylcholinesterase inhibitor neostigmine is used instead. Ondansetron is a 5-HT3 antagonist antiemetic that has no effect on gastric motility and may slow colonic transit, so it would not relieve pseudo-obstruction. Octreotide inhibits intestinal secretion and at higher doses inhibits motility, so it does not decompress a dilated colon. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Cholinomimetic Agents). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: neostigmine is a quaternary (charged) AChE inhibitor that does not enter the CNS and treats postoperative ileus and urinary retention (First Aid Autonomic Drugs (cholinomimetic agents)). COMLEX: cholinergic effects of the IV dose include salivation, vomiting and bradycardia (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 4 — CCK and GIP slow gastric emptying","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"E":"Dicyclomine is an antispasmodic for IBS cramping and would w","A":"The anticholinergic properties of atropine add to its antidi","C":"Ondansetron is a 5-HT3 antagonist antiemetic that has no eff","B":"Octreotide inhibits intestinal secretion and at higher doses"}},{"id":"sdl37_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the mechanisms of action, clinical uses, and important adverse effects of medications (prokinetic and cholinomimetic agents) used to stimulate gastrointestinal motility.","batch":1,"stem":"Why does domperidone cause extrapyramidal effects far less often than metoclopramide?","choices":{"A":"Poor blood-brain barrier entry","B":"Faster renal clearance of the drug","C":"Weaker binding at gut D2 receptors","D":"Added serotonin 5-HT4 agonism","E":"Greater pituitary prolactin release"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Domperidone shares the D2-blocking mechanism but crosses the blood-brain barrier poorly, so central extrapyramidal effects are rare. Domperidone has the same peripheral D2-blocking prokinetic mechanism as metoclopramide, so weaker gut binding is not the reason. Domperidone is a D2 antagonist without 5-HT4 agonism; its CNS sparing reflects poor blood-brain barrier entry. Domperidone still causes hyperprolactinemia, which does not explain fewer extrapyramidal effects. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Dopamine Receptor Antagonists). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Katzung) — detail not in SDL 37 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: centrally acting D2 blockers cause parkinsonism and acute dystonia as drug-induced movement disorders (First Aid Gastrointestinal Pharmacology). COMLEX: domperidone still acts at the area postrema, which lies outside the barrier (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 4 — CCK and GIP slow gastric emptying","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Katzung","whyNot":{"C":"Domperidone has the same peripheral D2-blocking prokinetic m","D":"Domperidone is a D2 antagonist without 5-HT4 agonism; its CN","E":"Domperidone still causes hyperprolactinemia, which does not "}},{"id":"sdl37_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare and contrast the pharmacologic therapies for irritable bowel syndrome (IBS) based on the predominant symptom pattern, including IBS with diarrhea, IBS with constipation, and mixed-type IBS.","batch":1,"stem":"Alosetron use for diarrhea-predominant IBS is restricted mainly because of what serious adverse effect?","choices":{"A":"Torsades de pointes","B":"Ischemic colitis","C":"Agranulocytosis","D":"QT shortening","E":"Hepatic necrosis"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Alosetron, a 5-HT3 antagonist, causes constipation in up to 30% and ischemic colitis in up to 3 per 1000 patients, so it is restricted to women with severe IBS with diarrhea. Torsades and cardiac toxicity were the problem with older 5-HT4 agonists like cisapride, not alosetron. Agranulocytosis is the classic serious toxicity of clozapine, not of the 5-HT3 antagonist alosetron. Hepatic necrosis is reported with drugs such as colchicine; alosetron is restricted for constipation complications and ischemic colitis. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Drugs Used in the Treatment of Irritable Bowel Syndrome). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: 5-HT3 antagonists such as ondansetron are antiemetics whose adverse effects include headache and constipation (First Aid Gastrointestinal Pharmacology). COMLEX: alosetron blocks 5-HT3 receptors on extrinsic afferents that carry visceral pain signals (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 → Obj 1 — irritable bowel syndrome diagnosis and first-line treatment","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Torsades and cardiac toxicity were the problem with older 5-","C":"Agranulocytosis is the classic serious toxicity of clozapine","E":"Hepatic necrosis is reported with drugs such as colchicine; "}},{"id":"sdl37_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare and contrast the pharmacologic therapies for irritable bowel syndrome (IBS) based on the predominant symptom pattern, including IBS with diarrhea, IBS with constipation, and mixed-type IBS.","batch":1,"stem":"Lubiprostone relieves constipation-predominant IBS by which mechanism?","choices":{"A":"ClC-2 chloride channel activation","B":"Mu opioid receptor activation in gut","C":"Na+/K+ ATPase pump inhibition","D":"Bulk retention of stool water","E":"5-HT3 receptor inhibition on afferents"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Lubiprostone, a prostanoic acid derivative, activates type 2 chloride channels (ClC-2) on the apical enterocyte membrane, increasing chloride-rich fluid secretion into the lumen. Mu opioid activation, as with loperamide, slows transit and treats diarrhea, not constipation. Bulk retention of stool water describes fiber such as psyllium, not lubiprostone. 5-HT3 inhibition is alosetron's mechanism for IBS-D and causes constipation. Na+/K+-ATPase inhibition is the action of digoxin on the heart, not of lubiprostone. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Chloride Channel Activator). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: lubiprostone appears in the anticonstipation table with diarrhea and nausea as adverse effects (First Aid Gastrointestinal Pharmacology). COMLEX: lubiprostone is approved for women with IBS-C and avoided in women who may become pregnant (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 → Obj 1 — irritable bowel syndrome diagnosis and first-line treatment","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Mu opioid activation, as with loperamide, slows transit and ","D":"Bulk retention of stool water describes fiber such as psylli","E":"5-HT3 inhibition is alosetron's mechanism for IBS-D and caus","C":"Na+/K+-ATPase inhibition is the action of digoxin on the hea"}},{"id":"sdl37_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare and contrast the pharmacologic therapies for irritable bowel syndrome (IBS) based on the predominant symptom pattern, including IBS with diarrhea, IBS with constipation, and mixed-type IBS.","batch":1,"stem":"Eluxadoline for diarrhea-predominant IBS should be avoided in a patient with which history?","choices":{"A":"Prior appendectomy","B":"Hypothyroidism","C":"Prior pancreatitis","D":"Recurrent gout attacks","E":"Migraine headaches"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Eluxadoline, a mu-opioid agonist, can cause sphincter of Oddi spasm with pancreatitis, so it is avoided with prior pancreatitis, alcoholism or sphincter of Oddi disease. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Antidiarrheal Agents). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: peripherally acting mu agonists slow gut motility with low addiction potential (First Aid Gastrointestinal Pharmacology). COMLEX: a lower 75 mg dose is used after cholecystectomy because sphincter of Oddi complications are more common (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 → Obj 1 — irritable bowel syndrome diagnosis and first-line treatment","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl37_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the mechanism of action and clinical use of teduglutide in the management of short-bowel syndrome","batch":1,"stem":"Teduglutide is an analog of which gut hormone?","choices":{"A":"Glucagon-like peptide 1","B":"Glucose insulinotropic peptide","C":"Cholecystokinin","D":"Glucagon-like peptide 2","E":"Pancreatic polypeptide"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Teduglutide is a GLP-2 analog used for short-bowel syndrome; GLP-2 is an intestinotrophic peptide released from L cells. GLP-1 receptor agonists such as exenatide are glucose-lowering drugs for diabetes, whereas teduglutide is a GLP-2 analog. Cholecystokinin is the hormone that stimulates gallbladder emptying, not the peptide mimicked by teduglutide. GIP is an incretin secreted by duodenal and jejunal K cells that stimulates insulin secretion; teduglutide mimics GLP-2, not GIP. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Glucagon-Like Peptide 2 Analog for Short-Bowel Syndrome). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: GLP-1 analogs such as exenatide are incretin drugs, unlike the intestinotrophic analog used for short gut (First Aid Endocrine Pharmacology). COMLEX: teduglutide binds receptors on enteric neurons and endocrine cells (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 3 — enterohepatic circulation and terminal ileal bile salt loss","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"GLP-1 receptor agonists such as exenatide are glucose-loweri","C":"Cholecystokinin is the hormone that stimulates gallbladder e","B":"GIP is an incretin secreted by duodenal and jejunal K cells "}},{"id":"sdl37_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the mechanism of action and clinical use of teduglutide in the management of short-bowel syndrome","batch":1,"stem":"By which route is teduglutide given for short-bowel syndrome?","choices":{"A":"Weekly intravenous infusion pump","B":"Twice-daily oral tablet","C":"Monthly intramuscular depot","D":"Daily rectal suppository","E":"Daily subcutaneous injection"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Teduglutide is given at 0.05 mg/kg once daily by subcutaneous injection. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Glucagon-Like Peptide 2 Analog for Short-Bowel Syndrome). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: teduglutide is a GLP-2 analog given by subcutaneous injection (Katzung Ch. 62). COMLEX: teduglutide dosing is weight based at 0.05 mg/kg per day (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 3 — enterohepatic circulation and terminal ileal bile salt loss","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl37_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the mechanism of action and clinical use of teduglutide in the management of short-bowel syndrome","batch":1,"stem":"Teduglutide helps patients with short-bowel syndrome mainly by promoting what?","choices":{"A":"Faster emptying of the stomach","B":"Slower colonic transit","C":"Mucosal epithelial growth","D":"Pancreatic enzyme output","E":"Hepatic bile acid output"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Through trophic hormones, teduglutide stimulates mucosal epithelial growth of the remaining bowel and enhances fluid absorption. Teduglutide actually delays gastric emptying rather than speeding it. Slower colonic transit is how the mu-opioid agonist eluxadoline treats diarrhea; teduglutide instead drives mucosal epithelial growth and fluid absorption. Pancreatic enzyme output is not the target; teduglutide releases trophic hormones that grow the remaining mucosa and enhance fluid absorption. Bile acid output is not the target; teduglutide releases trophic hormones that grow the remaining mucosa and enhance fluid absorption. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Glucagon-Like Peptide 2 Analog for Short-Bowel Syndrome). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: short-bowel syndrome causes malabsorption of nutrients and fluid after extensive resection (First Aid Gastrointestinal Pharmacology). COMLEX: teduglutide acts on enteric neurons and endocrine cells to adapt the residual gut (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 3 — enterohepatic circulation and terminal ileal bile salt loss","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Teduglutide actually delays gastric emptying rather than spe","B":"Slower colonic transit is how the mu-opioid agonist eluxadol","D":"Pancreatic enzyme output is not the target; teduglutide rele","E":"Bile acid output is not the target; teduglutide releases tro"}},{"id":"sdl37_q10","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the mechanisms of action, clinical uses, and important adverse effects of medications (prokinetic and cholinomimetic agents) used to stimulate gastrointestinal motility.","batch":2,"stem":"A 68-year-old woman with diabetic gastroparesis has taken metoclopramide for 2 years. She now has repetitive lip smacking and tongue thrusting that persist after the drug is stopped. Which adverse effect is this?","choices":{"A":"Acute dystonic reaction","B":"Akathisia restlessness","C":"Serotonin syndrome","D":"Tardive dyskinesia","E":"Essential tremor"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Long-term metoclopramide can cause tardive dyskinesia that may be irreversible, so prolonged use is avoided, especially in older adults. Acute dystonia occurs early in therapy, whereas persistent movements after long-term use indicate tardive dyskinesia. Akathisia is subjective restlessness, not the persistent orofacial movements of tardive dyskinesia. Serotonin syndrome is an acute hyperthermic syndrome from serotonergic drugs such as SSRIs, not persistent orofacial movements after long-term D2 blockade. Essential tremor is a familial postural tremor of the hands, head, or voice, not the persistent orofacial movements of long-term D2 blockade. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Dopamine Receptor Antagonists). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: delayed orofacial choreiform movements follow long-term D2 blockade by antipsychotics or prokinetics (First Aid Pharmacology (drug adverse effects)). COMLEX: tardive movements are more likely with long-term use in older patients (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 4 — CCK and GIP slow gastric emptying","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Acute dystonia occurs early in therapy, whereas persistent m","B":"Akathisia is subjective restlessness, not the persistent oro","C":"Serotonin syndrome is an acute hyperthermic syndrome from se","E":"Essential tremor is a familial postural tremor of the hands,"}},{"id":"sdl37_q11","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the mechanisms of action, clinical uses, and important adverse effects of medications (prokinetic and cholinomimetic agents) used to stimulate gastrointestinal motility.","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old woman takes metoclopramide for postsurgical gastroparesis. Months later she has milky nipple discharge and missed periods. Which laboratory finding is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Low serum prolactin","B":"High serum cortisol","C":"High serum prolactin","D":"Low free thyroxine level","E":"High serum calcium"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Dopamine tonically inhibits prolactin release; D2 blockade raises prolactin and can cause galactorrhea, amenorrhea and gynecomastia. Metoclopramide's D2 blockade raises prolactin, causing galactorrhea, not lowering it. Galactorrhea and menstrual disorders on metoclopramide come from elevated prolactin after D2 blockade, not from cortisol excess. Galactorrhea and menstrual disorders on metoclopramide come from elevated prolactin after D2 blockade, not from low thyroxine. Galactorrhea and menstrual disorders on metoclopramide come from elevated prolactin after D2 blockade, not from hypercalcemia. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Dopamine Receptor Antagonists). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: dopamine antagonists are a drug cause of hyperprolactinemia with galactorrhea (First Aid Gastrointestinal Pharmacology). COMLEX: domperidone is used off-label for postpartum lactation through the same pituitary effect (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 5 — enteric nervous system control of motility","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Metoclopramide's D2 blockade raises prolactin, causing galac","B":"Galactorrhea and menstrual disorders on metoclopramide come ","D":"Galactorrhea and menstrual disorders on metoclopramide come from elevated prolactin after D2 blockade, not from l","E":"Galactorrhea and menstrual disorders on metoclopramide come from elevated prolactin after D2 blockade, not from h"}},{"id":"sdl37_q12","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the mechanisms of action, clinical uses, and important adverse effects of medications (prokinetic and cholinomimetic agents) used to stimulate gastrointestinal motility.","batch":2,"stem":"A 50-year-old man with diabetic gastroparesis improves on oral erythromycin, which induces fasting migrating motor complexes. After several weeks the benefit fades despite the same dose. What best explains this?","choices":{"A":"Gastric acid breakdown of the drug","B":"Rapid development of tolerance","C":"Induced CYP3A4 metabolism","D":"Selection of resistant flora","E":"Worsening autonomic damage"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Erythromycin stimulates motilin receptors and initiates the migrating motor complex, which normally recurs about every 90 minutes during fasting; tolerance develops rapidly, limiting long-term use. Erythromycin inhibits rather than induces CYP3A, forming a metabolite complex that inactivates the enzyme. Oral erythromycin works at first in gastroparesis, so acid breakdown does not explain fading benefit; tolerance develops rapidly with chronic use. Erythromycin speeds emptying by directly stimulating motilin receptors on gut smooth muscle, so bacterial resistance does not affect this prokinetic action. Fading benefit at the same dose fits the rapid tolerance that limits chronic erythromycin, not progressive autonomic damage. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Macrolides). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Katzung) — detail not in SDL 37 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: macrolides inhibit CYP3A4 and cause QT prolongation as notable interactions (First Aid Antimicrobials (macrolides)). COMLEX: a single IV macrolide dose of 3 mg/kg can clear the stomach of blood before upper endoscopy (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 3 — migrating motor complex during fasting","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Katzung","whyNot":{"C":"Erythromycin inhibits rather than induces CYP3A, forming a m","A":"Oral erythromycin works at first in gastroparesis, so acid b","D":"Erythromycin speeds emptying by directly stimulating motilin","E":"Fading benefit at the same dose fits the rapid tolerance tha"}},{"id":"sdl37_q13","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare and contrast the pharmacologic therapies for irritable bowel syndrome (IBS) based on the predominant symptom pattern, including IBS with diarrhea, IBS with constipation, and mixed-type IBS.","batch":2,"stem":"A 30-year-old woman has IBS with diarrhea: frequent loose stools and urgency but mild pain. She wants an inexpensive over-the-counter medicine. Which is most appropriate?","choices":{"A":"Loperamide","B":"Lubiprostone","C":"Psyllium","D":"Bisacodyl","E":"Neomycin"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Loperamide, a peripherally acting mu-opioid agonist, reduces stool frequency and urgency in IBS with diarrhea; it does not relieve abdominal pain. Lubiprostone is a prescription chloride channel activator for IBS-C and would worsen diarrhea. Bisacodyl is a stimulant laxative for constipation and would worsen diarrhea. Psyllium is a bulk-forming fiber, and colonic bacterial digestion of fiber can increase bloating and flatus. Neomycin is a poorly absorbed oral aminoglycoside used before bowel surgery or in hepatic encephalopathy, not an IBS-D remedy. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Drugs Used in the Treatment of Irritable Bowel Syndrome). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: antidiarrheal mu agonists have poor CNS penetration and cause constipation and nausea (First Aid Gastrointestinal Pharmacology). COMLEX: diphenoxylate is combined with atropine to discourage overdosage (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 → Obj 1 — irritable bowel syndrome diagnosis and first-line treatment","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Lubiprostone is a prescription chloride channel activator fo","D":"Bisacodyl is a stimulant laxative for constipation and would","C":"Psyllium is a bulk-forming fiber, and colonic bacterial dige","E":"Neomycin is a poorly absorbed oral aminoglycoside used befor"}},{"id":"sdl37_q14","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare and contrast the pharmacologic therapies for irritable bowel syndrome (IBS) based on the predominant symptom pattern, including IBS with diarrhea, IBS with constipation, and mixed-type IBS.","batch":2,"stem":"A 40-year-old man has IBS with constipation and bloating. Fiber and over-the-counter laxatives have not helped. He wants a capsule taken each morning that raises enterocyte cGMP. Which drug fits?","choices":{"A":"Lubiprostone","B":"Linaclotide","C":"Alosetron","D":"Eluxadoline","E":"Hyoscyamine"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Linaclotide (290 mcg daily) activates guanylyl cyclase C, raising cGMP, which opens CFTR and increases chloride and bicarbonate secretion; lubiprostone is approved in women. Alosetron is a 5-HT3 antagonist for IBS-D in women and would worsen constipation. Eluxadoline treats IBS-D and is constipating, so it suits diarrhea, not constipation. Hyoscyamine is an antispasmodic for acute cramping and does not raise cGMP. Lubiprostone activates the ClC-2 chloride channel rather than raising cGMP, and it is approved for IBS-C only in women. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Guanylate Cyclase C Agonists). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: GC-C agonists such as plecanatide cause diarrhea, bloating and flatulence (First Aid Gastrointestinal Pharmacology). COMLEX: GC-C agonists are contraindicated in children because of dehydration risk (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 3 — CFTR and intestinal chloride secretion","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Alosetron is a 5-HT3 antagonist for IBS-D in women and would","D":"Eluxadoline treats IBS-D and is constipating, so it suits di","E":"Hyoscyamine is an antispasmodic for acute cramping and does ","A":"Lubiprostone activates the ClC-2 chloride channel rather tha"}},{"id":"sdl37_q15","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare and contrast the pharmacologic therapies for irritable bowel syndrome (IBS) based on the predominant symptom pattern, including IBS with diarrhea, IBS with constipation, and mixed-type IBS.","batch":2,"stem":"A 36-year-old woman has mixed-type IBS, alternating stool form with chronic abdominal pain as her main complaint. Which nightly drug best targets the pain?","choices":{"A":"High-dose bismuth salts","B":"Daily lactulose syrup by mouth","C":"Oral metoclopramide","D":"Low-dose amitriptyline","E":"Weekly erythromycin"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Low-dose tricyclic antidepressants alter central processing of visceral afferent input and help chronic IBS pain; their anticholinergic effect also slows stools. Katzung does not separate mixed-type therapy, so this item is objectives-first. Lactulose is a nonabsorbable sugar laxative that colonic bacteria ferment into flatus and cramps, so it does not target IBS pain. Metoclopramide is a prokinetic and antiemetic, not a treatment for chronic visceral pain. Bismuth subsalicylate is used short term for dyspepsia and acute or traveler's diarrhea, not as chronic pain therapy for IBS. Erythromycin stimulates motilin receptors to treat gastroparesis, and tolerance develops rapidly; it does not relieve IBS pain. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Drugs Used in the Treatment of Irritable Bowel Syndrome). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: tricyclic antidepressants cause anticholinergic, sedative and cardiac conduction effects (First Aid Psychiatry Pharmacology). COMLEX: desipramine is dosed at 10 to 50 mg per day for chronic IBS pain (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 → Obj 1 — irritable bowel syndrome diagnosis and first-line treatment","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Lactulose is a nonabsorbable sugar laxative that colonic bac","C":"Metoclopramide is a prokinetic and antiemetic, not a treatme","A":"Bismuth subsalicylate is used short term for dyspepsia and a","E":"Erythromycin stimulates motilin receptors to treat gastropar"}},{"id":"sdl37_q16","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the mechanism of action and clinical use of teduglutide in the management of short-bowel syndrome","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old man had massive small-bowel resection after a volvulus and needs home intravenous nutrition 6 nights a week. Teduglutide is started. Which outcome best shows that it is working?","choices":{"A":"Higher fasting glucose","B":"Faster gastric emptying","C":"Lower serum prolactin level","D":"Rise in serum bilirubin","E":"Reduced infusion frequency"},"correct":"E","explanation":"In trials, 54% of patients given teduglutide cut parenteral support by at least 1 day per week, versus 23% given placebo. Teduglutide delays gastric emptying, and reduced parenteral support is the measure of benefit. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Glucagon-Like Peptide 2 Analog for Short-Bowel Syndrome). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: extensive resection removes absorptive surface and causes malabsorption of nutrients and fluids (First Aid Gastrointestinal Pharmacology). COMLEX: patients with under 200 cm of small intestine usually need partial or complete parenteral support (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 4 — pathogenesis of general malabsorption","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Teduglutide delays gastric emptying, and reduced parenteral "}},{"id":"sdl37_q17","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the mechanism of action and clinical use of teduglutide in the management of short-bowel syndrome","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old woman with short-bowel syndrome is being considered for teduglutide. Which screening is most important because of a known drug risk?","choices":{"A":"Echocardiography for valves","B":"Bone density scanning","C":"Colonoscopy for neoplasia","D":"Audiometry for hearing","E":"Slit-lamp eye examination"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Teduglutide is trophic to the mucosa and may raise the risk of colorectal and small-bowel neoplasia, so colonoscopy is done before and during therapy. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Glucagon-Like Peptide 2 Analog for Short-Bowel Syndrome). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: trophic stimulation of intestinal epithelium raises concern for adenomatous growth (Katzung Ch. 62). COMLEX: GLP-2 analog therapy carries a neoplasia warning for colon and small bowel (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 1 — colorectal adenomas and malignant potential","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl37_q18","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the mechanism of action and clinical use of teduglutide in the management of short-bowel syndrome","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old man with short-bowel syndrome after Crohn resections starts teduglutide. Its effect on the remaining mucosa is mediated downstream by release of which hormone?","choices":{"A":"Insulin-like growth factor","B":"Histamine from mast cells","C":"Somatostatin from D cells","D":"Substance P from neurons","E":"Gastrin from antral G cells"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Teduglutide binds enteric neurons and endocrine cells and stimulates release of trophic hormones, including insulin-like growth factor, that drive epithelial growth and fluid absorption. Gastrin from antral G cells stimulates parietal cell acid secretion, not the trophic response of small-bowel mucosa to teduglutide. Somatostatin, mimicked by octreotide, inhibits intestinal secretion and hormone release rather than driving mucosal growth. Histamine from ECL cells mediates gastrin-driven acid secretion via parietal H2 receptors, not GLP-2 mucosal growth. Teduglutide acts through trophic hormones such as insulin-like growth factor, not substance P, to drive mucosal epithelial growth. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Glucagon-Like Peptide 2 Analog for Short-Bowel Syndrome). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: VIP from enteric neurons increases intestinal water and electrolyte secretion (First Aid Gastrointestinal Physiology (GI regulatory substances)). COMLEX: octreotide may be used for high-output diarrhea in short-bowel syndrome (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 5 — enteric nervous system control of motility","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"E":"Gastrin from antral G cells stimulates parietal cell acid se","C":"Somatostatin, mimicked by octreotide, inhibits intestinal se","B":"Histamine from ECL cells mediates gastrin-driven acid secret","D":"Teduglutide acts through trophic hormones such as insulin-li"}},{"id":"trial_sdl37_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the mechanisms of action, clinical uses, and important adverse effects of medications (prokinetic and cholinomimetic agents) used to stimulate gastrointestinal motility.","batch":3,"stem":"A 72-year-old man has urinary retention and slow gut transit. Why is bethanechol seldom used for GI motility today?","choices":{"A":"Rapid loss of the drug's effect","B":"Frequent tardive movement disorders","C":"Widespread cholinergic actions","D":"Serious hepatic toxicity","E":"Marked drop in white blood cells"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Bethanechol stimulates M3 receptors on muscle and myenteric neurons but is seldom used for GI motility because of its broad cholinergic effects and newer alternatives. Tardive dyskinesia is the black box warning for metoclopramide, a dopamine receptor antagonist. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Cholinomimetic Agents). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the -chol suffix marks a direct muscarinic agonist resistant to AChE that treats urinary retention (First Aid Autonomic Drugs (cholinomimetic agents)). COMLEX: bethanechol still sees occasional use for postoperative or neurogenic bladder retention (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 5 — enteric nervous system control of motility","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Tardive dyskinesia is the black box warning for metocloprami"}},{"id":"trial_sdl37_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the mechanisms of action, clinical uses, and important adverse effects of medications (prokinetic and cholinomimetic agents) used to stimulate gastrointestinal motility.","batch":3,"stem":"A 55-year-old woman with chronic constipation and long QT starts prucalopride. Why is it safer than older 5-HT4 agonists?","choices":{"A":"Little CYP3A4 hepatic metabolism","B":"Little hERG channel affinity","C":"Strong D2 receptor blockade","D":"Shorter plasma half-life","E":"Partial muscarinic blockade"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Cisapride and tegaserod were withdrawn for cardiovascular events; prucalopride is a high-affinity 5-HT4 agonist without significant hERG K+ channel or 5-HT1B affinity. Dopamine receptor blockade is the mechanism of metoclopramide and domperidone, not of 5-HT4 agonists. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Serotonin 5-HT4-Receptor Agonists). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: 5-HT4 agonism stimulates enteric nerves to increase peristalsis and secretion (First Aid Gastrointestinal Pharmacology). COMLEX: prucalopride dosing falls to 1 mg daily when creatinine clearance is below 30 mL/min (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 5 — enteric nervous system control of motility","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Dopamine receptor blockade is the mechanism of metoclopramid"}},{"id":"trial_sdl37_q03","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare and contrast the pharmacologic therapies for irritable bowel syndrome (IBS) based on the predominant symptom pattern, including IBS with diarrhea, IBS with constipation, and mixed-type IBS.","batch":3,"stem":"A 26-year-old woman with IBS with constipation bloats badly on psyllium. Which inexpensive agent is a sensible next step?","choices":{"A":"Diphenoxylate tablets","B":"Alosetron tablets","C":"Cholestyramine","D":"Polyethylene glycol","E":"Bismuth subsalicylate"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Fiber can worsen bloating in IBS with constipation; osmotic laxatives such as polyethylene glycol or milk of magnesia soften stools and are often tried next. Diphenoxylate is a mu-opioid agonist antidiarrheal that slows colonic transit and would worsen constipation. Alosetron inhibits colonic motility and extends transit, so it suits diarrhea-predominant rather than constipation-predominant IBS. Cholestyramine treats bile acid diarrhea and commonly causes constipation and bloating. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Drugs Used in the Treatment of Irritable Bowel Syndrome). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: osmotic laxatives draw water into the lumen and can cause diarrhea and dehydration (First Aid Gastrointestinal Pharmacology). COMLEX: bulk-forming fiber can increase gas and distension in IBS (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 → Obj 1 — irritable bowel syndrome diagnosis and first-line treatment","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Diphenoxylate is a mu-opioid agonist antidiarrheal that slow","B":"Alosetron inhibits colonic motility and extends transit, so ","C":"Cholestyramine treats bile acid diarrhea and commonly causes"}},{"id":"trial_sdl37_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare and contrast the pharmacologic therapies for irritable bowel syndrome (IBS) based on the predominant symptom pattern, including IBS with diarrhea, IBS with constipation, and mixed-type IBS.","batch":3,"stem":"A 70-year-old man with IBS cramping takes dicyclomine. Which adverse effect is most likely at higher doses?","choices":{"A":"Slow heart rate","B":"Excess salivation","C":"Blurred near vision","D":"Loose watery stools","E":"Pinpoint pupils"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Dicyclomine and hyoscyamine are antimuscarinic antispasmodics that give short-term cramp relief; higher doses cause dry mouth, blurred near vision and constipation. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Drugs Used in the Treatment of Irritable Bowel Syndrome). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: muscarinic antagonists cause dry mouth, mydriasis and cycloplegia (First Aid Autonomic Drugs (cholinomimetic agents)). COMLEX: antispasmodic benefit in IBS is short term and modest (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 5 — enteric nervous system control of motility","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl37_q05","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the mechanism of action and clinical use of teduglutide in the management of short-bowel syndrome","batch":3,"stem":"A 47-year-old woman had extensive small-bowel resection. Below what residual length do such patients usually need IV nutrition?","choices":{"A":"Less than 20 cm","B":"Less than 600 cm","C":"Less than 400 cm","D":"Less than 200 cm","E":"Less than 50 cm"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Patients with less than 200 cm of small intestine, with or without colon resection, usually depend on partial or complete parenteral support. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Glucagon-Like Peptide 2 Analog for Short-Bowel Syndrome). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: loss of absorptive length impairs absorption of fluids and nutrients (First Aid Gastrointestinal Pharmacology). COMLEX: the adult small intestine is several meters long, so short-bowel syndrome reflects major loss (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 4 — pathogenesis of general malabsorption","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl37_q06","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the mechanism of action and clinical use of teduglutide in the management of short-bowel syndrome","batch":3,"stem":"A 39-year-old man with short-bowel syndrome starts teduglutide. Which additional effect of the drug reduces his stool output?","choices":{"A":"Enhanced fluid absorption","B":"Faster colonic transit","C":"Reduced hepatic bile flow","D":"Increased mucus output","E":"Greater gut colonization"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Teduglutide stimulates trophic hormone release that promotes epithelial growth and enhances intestinal fluid absorption. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Glucagon-Like Peptide 2 Analog for Short-Bowel Syndrome). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: volume of fluid lost after resection reflects reduced absorptive surface (First Aid Gastrointestinal Pharmacology). COMLEX: GLP-2 receptors on enteric neurons and endocrine cells mediate the trophic response (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 3 — enterohepatic circulation and terminal ileal bile salt loss","isHighYield":true},{"id":"grid_sdl37_q01","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare and contrast the pharmacologic therapies for irritable bowel syndrome (IBS) based on the predominant symptom pattern, including IBS with diarrhea, IBS with constipation, and mixed-type IBS.","batch":4,"stem":"A 34-year-old woman has had 3 years of urgent loose stools with crampy lower abdominal pain relieved by defecation. Colonoscopy and celiac serologies are normal. Loperamide and dicyclomine did not control her symptoms. She is enrolled in a restricted prescribing program and begins a new drug. Six weeks later she has sudden left-sided abdominal pain and bloody diarrhea, and colonoscopy shows segmental mucosal edema and ulceration consistent with ischemic colitis. Which of the following sets of effects best characterizes the drug she was given?","grid":{"headers":["Colonic transit","Intestinal fluid secretion","Visceral pain"]},"choices":{"A":["↑","↑","↓"],"B":["↑","↑","↔"],"C":["↓","↔","↔"],"D":["↑","↔","↔"],"E":["↓","↔","↓"]},"correct":"E","explanation":"Choice E (↓ colonic transit, ↔ intestinal fluid secretion, ↓ visceral pain) is correct. Alosetron is a selective 5-HT3 receptor antagonist approved only for women with severe diarrhea-predominant IBS refractory to conventional therapy. The notes describe blockade of 5-HT3 receptors on enteric cholinergic neurons, which slows colonic transit, and on visceral sensory afferents, which reduces pain, cramps, and urgency; severe constipation and ischemic colitis carry a black box warning and require a REMS program. Choice A (↑ colonic transit, ↑ fluid secretion, ↓ visceral pain) is linaclotide, a guanylyl cyclase-C agonist that raises cGMP and activates CFTR, accelerates colonic transit, and blunts visceral nociception in IBS-C. Choice B (↑ colonic transit, ↑ fluid secretion, ↔ visceral pain) is lubiprostone, which activates ClC-2 chloride channels to drive fluid secretion in IBS-C. Choice C (↓ colonic transit, ↔ fluid secretion, ↔ visceral pain) is loperamide, a mu-opioid agonist that slows transit and increases fluid reabsorption but does not relieve visceral pain. Choice D (↑ colonic transit, ↔ fluid secretion, ↔ visceral pain) is prucalopride, a 5-HT4 agonist that triggers high-amplitude propagating contractions in chronic constipation. Sources: Exam 3 lecture notes (SDL 37, sections 1 and 2: prokinetic agents and IBS therapy by subtype); Katzung Ch. 62 (drugs used in IBS). The unchanged secretion for alosetron, loperamide, and prucalopride and the unchanged pain for lubiprostone and prucalopride are inferred because the notes describe no such effect.","boardPrep":"Step 1: alosetron (5-HT3 antagonist) for severe IBS-D in women; black box warning for ischemic colitis and severe constipation (Exam 3 notes, SDL 37). COMLEX: IBS-C secretagogues (linaclotide, lubiprostone) cause diarrhea; IBS-D agents (alosetron, eluxadoline, loperamide) cause constipation.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 3 — CFTR-mediated Cl⁻ and water secretion","isHighYield":true}],"38":[{"id":"sdl38_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the mechanisms of action of medications used to treat constipation, including stool softeners, osmotic laxatives, and stimulant laxatives, and explain their clinical applications.","batch":1,"stem":"Docusate softens stool mainly by which mechanism?","choices":{"A":"Lowering surface tension","B":"Drawing water in by osmosis","C":"Stimulating enteric neurons","D":"Forming a bulky fiber gel","E":"Opening apical ion channels"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Docusate is a stool surfactant that lets water and lipids penetrate the stool; it is often given to hospitalized patients to minimize straining. Drawing water in by osmosis is the action of osmotic laxatives such as PEG and lactulose, not docusate. Stimulating enteric neurons is the action of stimulant laxatives such as senna and bisacodyl, not docusate. Forming a bulky fiber gel describes bulk laxatives such as psyllium, not the surfactant docusate. Opening apical ion channels describes secretagogues such as lubiprostone and linaclotide, not docusate. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Stool Surfactant Agents). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: emollient laxatives are surfactants that promote water entry into stool (First Aid Gastrointestinal Pharmacology). COMLEX: glycerin suppositories are another stool surfactant option given rectally (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 5 — osmotic versus secretory diarrhea","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Drawing water in by osmosis is the action of osmotic laxativ","C":"Stimulating enteric neurons is the action of stimulant laxat","D":"Forming a bulky fiber gel describes bulk laxatives such as p","E":"Opening apical ion channels describes secretagogues such as "}},{"id":"sdl38_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the mechanisms of action of medications used to treat constipation, including stool softeners, osmotic laxatives, and stimulant laxatives, and explain their clinical applications.","batch":1,"stem":"A woman has used an anthraquinone laxative daily for years. Which colonoscopy finding is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Pseudomembranes","B":"Cobblestone mucosa","C":"Melanosis coli","D":"Crypt abscesses","E":"Angiodysplasia"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Aloe, senna and cascara are hydrolyzed in the colon; chronic use causes brown colonic pigmentation called melanosis coli, without a proven link to colorectal cancer. Pseudomembranes indicate C. difficile colitis, not chronic anthraquinone use. Cobblestone mucosa is a feature of Crohn disease, not laxative use. Crypt abscesses indicate active colitis such as ulcerative colitis, not anthraquinone pigmentation. Chronic anthraquinone use causes brown pigmentation of the colon (melanosis coli), not angiodysplasia. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Anthraquinone Derivatives). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Katzung) — detail not in SDL 38 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: stimulant laxatives act by enteric nerve stimulation and colonic contraction, with diarrhea as an adverse effect (First Aid Gastrointestinal Pharmacology). COMLEX: oral anthraquinones act in 6 to 12 hours and rectal doses within 2 hours (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 5 — osmotic versus secretory diarrhea","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Katzung","whyNot":{"A":"Pseudomembranes indicate C. difficile colitis, not chronic a","B":"Cobblestone mucosa is a feature of Crohn disease, not laxati","D":"Crypt abscesses indicate active colitis such as ulcerative c","E":"Chronic anthraquinone use causes brown pigmentation of the c"}},{"id":"sdl38_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the mechanisms of action of medications used to treat constipation, including stool softeners, osmotic laxatives, and stimulant laxatives, and explain their clinical applications.","batch":1,"stem":"Long-term use of mineral oil as a lubricant laxative can cause which nutritional problem?","choices":{"A":"Copper deficiency anemia","B":"Iron overload in the liver","C":"Zinc deficiency rash","D":"Fat-soluble vitamin loss","E":"Excess vitamin C levels"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Mineral oil coats stool and retards water absorption; long-term use impairs absorption of vitamins A, D, E and K, and aspiration can cause lipid pneumonitis. Mineral oil impairs absorption of fat-soluble vitamins A, D, E and K; copper deficiency is not its nutritional risk. Mineral oil reduces absorption of fat-soluble vitamins A, D, E and K rather than causing hepatic iron overload. Mineral oil impairs absorption of fat-soluble vitamins A, D, E and K; zinc deficiency is not its nutritional risk. Mineral oil lowers absorption of fat-soluble vitamins (A, D, E, K); it does not raise water-soluble vitamin C levels. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Stool Surfactant Agents). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: absorption of fat-soluble vitamins A, D, E and K depends on bile emulsification, and mineral oil intake can cause their deficiency (First Aid Biochemistry, fat-soluble vitamins). COMLEX: mineral oil is used for fecal impaction in young children and debilitated adults (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 5 — intestinal absorption of fat-soluble vitamins and calcium","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Mineral oil impairs absorption of fat-soluble vitamins A, D,","B":"Mineral oil reduces absorption of fat-soluble vitamins A, D,","C":"Mineral oil impairs absorption of fat-soluble vitamins A, D, E and K; z","E":"Mineral oil lowers absorption of fat-soluble vitamins (A, D,"}},{"id":"sdl38_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare the mechanisms of action, therapeutic uses, and adverse effects of major classes of antidiarrheal medications, including opioid agonists, bile acid–binding resins, and octreotide, and identify clinical scenarios in which each is indicated.","batch":1,"stem":"Why do diphenoxylate tablets contain a small amount of atropine?","choices":{"A":"Enhancing pain relief","B":"Preventing nausea","C":"Discouraging abuse","D":"Delaying tolerance","E":"Speeding absorption"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Diphenoxylate is a prescription opioid agonist with CNS effects and dependence at higher doses; 0.025 mg atropine is added to discourage overdosage and may add to the antidiarrheal action. Diphenoxylate has no analgesic properties at standard doses, and the atropine is added to discourage overdosage, not to relieve pain. The small dose of atropine is there to discourage overdosage of the opioid diphenoxylate, not to prevent nausea. Atropine is added to discourage overdosage because high doses of diphenoxylate cause CNS opioid effects and dependence, not to delay tolerance. Atropine is added to discourage overdosage, and its anticholinergic action may add to the antidiarrheal effect; it is not there to speed absorption. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Opioid Agonists). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: diphenoxylate is a mu agonist that slows gut motility with constipation and nausea as adverse effects (First Aid Gastrointestinal Pharmacology). COMLEX: opioids slow colonic transit by inhibiting presynaptic cholinergic nerves in the enteric plexuses (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 4 — secretory diarrhea and cAMP-mediated electrolyte loss","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Diphenoxylate has no analgesic properties at standard doses,","B":"The small dose of atropine is there to discourage overdosage","D":"Atropine is added to discourage overdosage because high dose","E":"Atropine is added to discourage overdosage, and its antichol"}},{"id":"sdl38_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare the mechanisms of action, therapeutic uses, and adverse effects of major classes of antidiarrheal medications, including opioid agonists, bile acid–binding resins, and octreotide, and identify clinical scenarios in which each is indicated.","batch":1,"stem":"Long-term somatostatin-analog therapy for acromegaly most commonly causes which problem?","choices":{"A":"Kidney calcium oxalate stones","B":"Severe symptomatic hypercalcemia","C":"Sustained sinus tachycardia","D":"Hyperthyroid storm crisis","E":"Gallbladder sludge or stones"},"correct":"E","explanation":"By inhibiting gallbladder contractility and altering fat absorption, long-term octreotide causes sludge or gallstones in over 50% of patients; it can also cause steatorrhea, hyperglycemia, hypothyroidism and bradycardia. Octreotide causes bradycardia, not tachycardia. Octreotide is associated with hypothyroidism, not thyroid storm. Kidney stones are not a listed octreotide effect; its stones form in the gallbladder because it inhibits gallbladder contractility. Hypercalcemia is not a listed octreotide effect; its endocrine effects are hyper- or hypoglycemia and hypothyroidism. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Octreotide). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Katzung) — detail not in SDL 38 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: somatostatin analogs raise cholelithiasis risk through CCK inhibition (First Aid Gastrointestinal Pharmacology). COMLEX: a monthly intramuscular depot form of the somatostatin analog is available (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 4 — secretory diarrhea and cAMP-mediated electrolyte loss","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Katzung","whyNot":{"C":"Octreotide causes bradycardia, not tachycardia.","D":"Octreotide is associated with hypothyroidism, not thyroid st","A":"Kidney stones are not a listed octreotide effect; its stones","B":"Hypercalcemia is not a listed octreotide effect; its endocri"}},{"id":"sdl38_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare the mechanisms of action, therapeutic uses, and adverse effects of major classes of antidiarrheal medications, including opioid agonists, bile acid–binding resins, and octreotide, and identify clinical scenarios in which each is indicated.","batch":1,"stem":"Colestipol for chronic diarrhea should be taken at least 2 hours apart from other medicines. Why?","choices":{"A":"It induces hepatic CYP3A4","B":"It binds many oral drugs","C":"It raises gastric pH","D":"It blocks P-glycoprotein","E":"It speeds gastric emptying"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Cholestyramine and colestipol bind many drugs and reduce their absorption, so they are separated from other drugs by 2 hours; colesevelam has little effect on drug absorption. Colestipol is a resin that is not absorbed, so it does not induce hepatic CYP3A4. Raising intragastric pH is how antacids alter drug dissolution, whereas colestipol is a resin that binds drugs. Enhanced gastric emptying is the prokinetic action of metoclopramide and domperidone, not colestipol. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Bile Salt-Binding Resins). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: bile acid resins cause GI upset and decreased absorption of other drugs (First Aid Cardiovascular Pharmacology, lipid-lowering agents). COMLEX: resin adverse effects include bloating, flatulence, constipation and fecal impaction (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 4 — secretory diarrhea and cAMP-mediated electrolyte loss","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Colestipol is a resin that is not absorbed, so it does not i","C":"Raising intragastric pH is how antacids alter drug dissoluti","E":"Enhanced gastric emptying is the prokinetic action of metocl"}},{"id":"sdl38_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Compare the major pharmacologic therapies used to treat ulcerative colitis and Crohn’s disease, including aminosalicylates, glucocorticoids, purine analogs, and anti-tumor necrosis factor therapy with emphasis on their mechanisms of action, therapeutic roles, and key adverse effects.","batch":1,"stem":"Sulfasalazine releases active 5-aminosalicylic acid in the colon through which process?","choices":{"A":"Bacterial azoreductase cleavage","B":"Hepatic CYP450 oxidation of it","C":"Gastric acid hydrolysis of the bond","D":"Pancreatic esterase cleavage","E":"pH-dependent resin release"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Sulfasalazine links 5-ASA to sulfapyridine by an azo bond; colonic bacteria cleave it with azoreductase, releasing 5-ASA in the terminal ileum and colon. pH-dependent release describes delayed-release mesalamine, whereas sulfasalazine is an azo compound cleaved by colonic bacteria. Sulfasalazine is not activated by hepatic CYP450; its azo bond is cleaved by bacterial azoreductase in the terminal ileum and colon. Gastric acid does not split the azo bond; the azo structure keeps the parent drug intact and poorly absorbed until colonic bacteria cleave it. Pancreatic esterases do not release 5-ASA from sulfasalazine; resident colonic bacteria cleave its azo (N=N) bond. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Aminosalicylates). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Katzung) — detail not in SDL 38 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: sulfasalazine combines antibacterial sulfapyridine with anti-inflammatory 5-ASA and is activated by colonic bacteria (First Aid Gastrointestinal Pharmacology). COMLEX: mesalamine products instead use timed-release granules or pH-sensitive coatings (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 → Obj 2 — differentiating the inflammatory bowel diseases","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Katzung","whyNot":{"E":"pH-dependent release describes delayed-release mesalamine, w","B":"Sulfasalazine is not activated by hepatic CYP450; its azo bo","C":"Gastric acid does not split the azo bond; the azo structure ","D":"Pancreatic esterases do not release 5-ASA from sulfasalazine"}},{"id":"sdl38_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Compare the major pharmacologic therapies used to treat ulcerative colitis and Crohn’s disease, including aminosalicylates, glucocorticoids, purine analogs, and anti-tumor necrosis factor therapy with emphasis on their mechanisms of action, therapeutic roles, and key adverse effects.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient starting sulfasalazine for ulcerative colitis should take which daily supplement?","choices":{"A":"Vitamin B6","B":"Vitamin D3","C":"Iron sulfate","D":"Folic acid","E":"Zinc salts"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Sulfasalazine impairs folate absorption and processing, so 1 mg/d folic acid is recommended. Pyridoxine (vitamin B6) is given with isoniazid to prevent its neuropathy, not with sulfasalazine. Sulfasalazine impairs folate absorption and processing, so folic acid, not vitamin D3, is the recommended daily supplement. Sulfasalazine impairs folate absorption and processing, so folic acid, not iron sulfate, is the recommended daily supplement. Sulfasalazine impairs folate absorption and processing, so folic acid, not zinc, is the recommended daily supplement. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Aminosalicylates). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Katzung) — detail not in SDL 38 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: sulfasalazine adverse effects include malaise, nausea, sulfonamide toxicity and reversible oligospermia (First Aid Gastrointestinal Pharmacology). COMLEX: slow acetylators of sulfapyridine have more frequent and severe adverse effects (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 → Obj 2 — differentiating the inflammatory bowel diseases","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Katzung","whyNot":{"A":"Pyridoxine (vitamin B6) is given with isoniazid to prevent i","B":"Sulfasalazine impairs folate absorption and processing, so f","C":"Sulfasalazine impairs folate absorption and processing, so folic acid, not i","E":"Sulfasalazine impairs folate absorption and processing, so folic acid, not z"}},{"id":"sdl38_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Compare the major pharmacologic therapies used to treat ulcerative colitis and Crohn’s disease, including aminosalicylates, glucocorticoids, purine analogs, and anti-tumor necrosis factor therapy with emphasis on their mechanisms of action, therapeutic roles, and key adverse effects.","batch":1,"stem":"Before starting azathioprine for Crohn disease, which test helps predict severe marrow toxicity?","choices":{"A":"G6PD enzyme activity","B":"TPMT enzyme activity","C":"HLA-B*5701 typing","D":"Serum ferritin level","E":"CYP2C19 genotype"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Thiopurine methyltransferase catabolizes 6-MP; activity is low in 11% and absent in 0.3% of people, raising active thioguanine nucleotides and marrow suppression risk. G6PD activity predicts oxidant hemolysis of red cells, not thiopurine marrow toxicity. HLA-B*5701 screening is done before abacavir to prevent hypersensitivity, not before azathioprine. CYP2C19 genotype predicts poor activation of clopidogrel, not thiopurine toxicity. Serum ferritin reflects stored body iron, not the TPMT catabolism of thiopurines that predicts marrow toxicity. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Purine Analogs). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Katzung) — detail not in SDL 38 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: azathioprine is an antimetabolite precursor of 6-mercaptopurine that causes pancytopenia (First Aid Immunology (immunosuppressants and therapeutic antibodies)). COMLEX: complete blood counts and liver tests are monitored in every patient on purine analogs (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 → Obj 2 — differentiating the inflammatory bowel diseases","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Katzung","whyNot":{"A":"G6PD activity predicts oxidant hemolysis of red cells, not t","C":"HLA-B*5701 screening is done before abacavir to prevent hype","E":"CYP2C19 genotype predicts poor activation of clopidogrel, no","D":"Serum ferritin reflects stored body iron, not the TPMT catab"}},{"id":"sdl38_q10","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the mechanisms of action of medications used to treat constipation, including stool softeners, osmotic laxatives, and stimulant laxatives, and explain their clinical applications.","batch":2,"stem":"A 74-year-old man with stage 4 chronic kidney disease has taken milk of magnesia nightly for months. He now has weakness, absent reflexes and bradycardia. Which disturbance is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Hypercalcemia","B":"Hypokalemia","C":"Hypermagnesemia","D":"Hyperphosphatemia","E":"Hypophosphatemia"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Magnesium hydroxide should not be used for long periods in renal insufficiency because of the risk of hypermagnesemia. Hypokalemia is listed among the electrolyte risks of sodium phosphate preparations, whereas magnesium hydroxide retained in renal insufficiency causes hypermagnesemia. Hyperphosphatemia is a risk of sodium phosphate purgatives, not of magnesium hydroxide. Hypercalcemia is the risk of excess calcium carbonate taken with dairy products (milk-alkali syndrome), not of magnesium hydroxide. Phosphate disturbances belong to sodium phosphate preparations, which cause hyperphosphatemia; milk of magnesia in renal insufficiency causes hypermagnesemia. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Osmotic Laxatives). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Katzung) — detail not in SDL 38 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: magnesium-containing laxatives provide an osmotic load and can cause diarrhea and dehydration (First Aid Gastrointestinal Pharmacology). COMLEX: the colon neither concentrates nor dilutes fecal fluid, so nonabsorbed solute obligates stool water (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 5 — osmotic versus secretory diarrhea","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Katzung","whyNot":{"B":"Hypokalemia is listed among the electrolyte risks of sodium ","D":"Hyperphosphatemia is a risk of sodium phosphate purgatives, ","A":"Hypercalcemia is the risk of excess calcium carbonate taken ","E":"Phosphate disturbances belong to sodium phosphate preparatio"}},{"id":"sdl38_q11","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the mechanisms of action of medications used to treat constipation, including stool softeners, osmotic laxatives, and stimulant laxatives, and explain their clinical applications.","batch":2,"stem":"A frail 79-year-old woman with mild renal insufficiency takes sodium phosphate tablets for colonoscopy preparation. A week later her creatinine has doubled. Biopsy would most likely show which change?","choices":{"A":"Urate nephropathy","B":"Nephrocalcinosis","C":"Oxalate nephropathy","D":"Myoglobin casts","E":"Light-chain cast nephropathy"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Sodium phosphate can cause hyperphosphatemia and hypocalcemia, and acute renal failure from tubular calcium phosphate deposition (nephrocalcinosis); it is avoided in frail, elderly or renal patients. Urate nephropathy is uric acid crystal obstruction of collecting ducts, typical of treated leukemia or lymphoma, not phosphate preparations. Oxalate crystals in the tubules are typical of ethylene glycol poisoning, not sodium phosphate exposure. Light-chain cast nephropathy results from Bence-Jones proteins in multiple myeloma, not bowel preparation. Sodium phosphate injures the kidney through tubular deposition of calcium phosphate (nephrocalcinosis), not myoglobin casts. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Osmotic Laxatives). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Katzung) — detail not in SDL 38 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: osmotic agents draw water into the lumen and can be misused by patients with bulimia (First Aid Gastrointestinal Pharmacology). COMLEX: balanced isotonic lavage solutions avoid intravascular fluid and electrolyte shifts during preparation (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 5 — osmotic versus secretory diarrhea","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Katzung","whyNot":{"A":"Urate nephropathy is uric acid crystal obstruction of collec","C":"Oxalate crystals in the tubules are typical of ethylene glyc","E":"Light-chain cast nephropathy results from Bence-Jones protei","D":"Sodium phosphate injures the kidney through tubular depositi"}},{"id":"sdl38_q12","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the mechanisms of action of medications used to treat constipation, including stool softeners, osmotic laxatives, and stimulant laxatives, and explain their clinical applications.","batch":2,"stem":"An 83-year-old bed-bound nursing home resident has had no bowel movement for 4 days. Staff want a stimulant laxative that works within an hour. Which is most appropriate?","choices":{"A":"Oral senna tablets","B":"Oral psyllium powder","C":"Oral docusate capsule","D":"Bisacodyl suppository","E":"Oral lactulose syrup"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Bisacodyl, a diphenylmethane stimulant, works in 30 to 60 minutes rectally versus 6 to 10 hours orally, with minimal systemic absorption. Oral senna is a stimulant but acts over hours, not within an hour like rectal bisacodyl. Psyllium is a bulk-forming fiber, not a stimulant, and acts slowly. Docusate is a stool softener used to prevent straining, not a fast-acting stimulant. Lactulose is an osmotic laxative, not a stimulant, and does not act within an hour. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Diphenylmethane Derivatives). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: stimulant laxatives cause colonic contraction through enteric nerve stimulation (First Aid Gastrointestinal Pharmacology). COMLEX: long-term stimulant use is probably safe and may be needed in neurologically impaired patients (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 5 — defecation reflex and anal sphincter control","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Oral senna is a stimulant but acts over hours, not within an","B":"Psyllium is a bulk-forming fiber, not a stimulant, and acts ","C":"Docusate is a stool softener used to prevent straining, not ","E":"Lactulose is an osmotic laxative, not a stimulant, and does "}},{"id":"sdl38_q13","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare the mechanisms of action, therapeutic uses, and adverse effects of major classes of antidiarrheal medications, including opioid agonists, bile acid–binding resins, and octreotide, and identify clinical scenarios in which each is indicated.","batch":2,"stem":"A 41-year-old man had 90 cm of terminal ileum resected for Crohn disease. He now has watery postprandial diarrhea without steatorrhea. Which drug best treats it?","choices":{"A":"Cholestyramine","B":"Pancrelipase","C":"Ondansetron","D":"Diphenoxylate","E":"Metronidazole tablets"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Ileal disease or resection causes malabsorbed bile salts that trigger colonic secretory diarrhea; bile salt-binding resins reduce it, though they can worsen fat malabsorption if the bile pool is depleted. Diphenoxylate slows transit nonspecifically but does not bind the malabsorbed bile acids driving this secretory diarrhea. Pancrelipase replaces enzymes for exocrine pancreatic insufficiency with steatorrhea, not bile acid diarrhea after ileal resection. Ondansetron is a 5-HT3 antagonist antiemetic for chemotherapy and postoperative emesis and does not bind bile acids. Metronidazole targets anaerobic bacteria and sensitive protozoa, which do not cause bile acid diarrhea after ileal resection. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Bile Salt-Binding Resins). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Katzung) — detail not in SDL 38 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: bile acid resins prevent intestinal reabsorption of bile acids, prompting the liver to use cholesterol to make more (First Aid Cardiovascular Pharmacology, lipid-lowering agents). COMLEX: resin powders are taken one to three times daily before meals (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 3 — enterohepatic circulation and terminal ileal bile salt loss","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Katzung","whyNot":{"D":"Diphenoxylate slows transit nonspecifically but does not bin","B":"Pancrelipase replaces enzymes for exocrine pancreatic insuff","C":"Ondansetron is a 5-HT3 antagonist antiemetic for chemotherap","E":"Metronidazole targets anaerobic bacteria and sensitive proto"}},{"id":"sdl38_q14","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare the mechanisms of action, therapeutic uses, and adverse effects of major classes of antidiarrheal medications, including opioid agonists, bile acid–binding resins, and octreotide, and identify clinical scenarios in which each is indicated.","batch":2,"stem":"A 57-year-old woman with metastatic carcinoid tumor has flushing, wheezing and voluminous watery diarrhea. Her liver lesions cannot be removed surgically. Which drug best controls her symptoms?","choices":{"A":"Diphenoxylate","B":"Octreotide","C":"Bismuth","D":"Lubiprostone","E":"Prednisone"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Octreotide inhibits hormone secretion by carcinoid and VIPoma tumors, reducing secretory diarrhea and flushing, and may slow tumor progression. Diphenoxylate slows transit but does not inhibit tumor hormone secretion or control carcinoid flushing. Lubiprostone stimulates intestinal chloride secretion and would worsen diarrhea. Bismuth subsalicylate reduces stool frequency in acute infectious diarrhea and does not inhibit carcinoid hormone secretion. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Octreotide). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: carcinoid syndrome is diagnosed by urinary 5-HIAA and treated with a somatostatin analog (First Aid Endocrine, carcinoid syndrome). COMLEX: higher subcutaneous doses of 100 to 250 mcg inhibit motility in dumping or vagotomy diarrhea (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 4 — secretory diarrhea and cAMP-mediated electrolyte loss","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Diphenoxylate slows transit but does not inhibit tumor hormo","D":"Lubiprostone stimulates intestinal chloride secretion and wo","C":"Bismuth subsalicylate reduces stool frequency in acute infec"}},{"id":"sdl38_q15","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare the mechanisms of action, therapeutic uses, and adverse effects of major classes of antidiarrheal medications, including opioid agonists, bile acid–binding resins, and octreotide, and identify clinical scenarios in which each is indicated.","batch":2,"stem":"A 29-year-old man has bloody diarrhea, a temperature of 39.4°C and abdominal tenderness after eating undercooked chicken. Why should loperamide be avoided?","choices":{"A":"Risk of central opioid dependence","B":"Risk of respiratory depression in adults","C":"Risk of severe serotonin syndrome","D":"Risk of worsened underlying illness","E":"Risk of tardive movement disorder"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Antidiarrheals are safe for mild to moderate acute diarrhea but should not be used with bloody diarrhea, high fever or systemic toxicity because they may worsen the underlying condition. Loperamide does not cross the blood-brain barrier, so central dependence is not the concern. Loperamide does not cross the blood-brain barrier, so respiratory depression is not the concern. Serotonin syndrome arises from serotonergic interactions such as SSRIs with MAOIs, not from loperamide. Tardive dyskinesia follows prolonged central dopamine blockade by drugs such as metoclopramide, not opioid antidiarrheals like loperamide. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Antidiarrheal Agents). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: peripheral mu agonists have poor CNS penetration and low addictive potential (First Aid Gastrointestinal Pharmacology). COMLEX: antidiarrheals are stopped when diarrhea worsens despite therapy (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 31 → Obj 1 — bacterial enterocolitis, including Yersinia mimicking appendicitis","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Loperamide does not cross the blood-brain barrier, so centra","B":"Loperamide does not cross the blood-brain barrier, so respir","C":"Serotonin syndrome arises from serotonergic interactions suc","E":"Tardive dyskinesia follows prolonged central dopamine blocka"}},{"id":"sdl38_q16","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Compare the major pharmacologic therapies used to treat ulcerative colitis and Crohn’s disease, including aminosalicylates, glucocorticoids, purine analogs, and anti-tumor necrosis factor therapy with emphasis on their mechanisms of action, therapeutic roles, and key adverse effects.","batch":2,"stem":"A 35-year-old woman with Crohn disease is maintained on azathioprine. She is started on allopurinol for gout and 3 weeks later has a white cell count of 1,100/µL (reference range: 4,500–11,000/µL). What explains this?","choices":{"A":"Reduced renal drug excretion","B":"Increased drug absorption","C":"Reduced 6-MP breakdown","D":"Allopurinol marrow allergy","E":"Folate antagonism"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Allopurinol blocks xanthine oxidase catabolism of the purine analogs, increasing active 6-thioguanine nucleotides and causing severe leukopenia. Folate antagonism is a sulfasalazine concern, whereas allopurinol blocks xanthine oxidase breakdown of azathioprine. Renal excretion clears only small amounts of unchanged azathioprine and 6-MP; most of the drug is inactivated by xanthine oxidase to 6-thiouric acid, the step allopurinol blocks. Azathioprine is already well absorbed from the gut; allopurinol raises its toxicity by blocking xanthine oxidase catabolism of 6-MP. This is predictable dose-related toxicity, not allergy: with allopurinol the azathioprine dose must be cut to one-fourth to one-third to prevent excess marrow suppression. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Purine Analogs). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: 6-MP is degraded by xanthine oxidase, so its toxicity rises with allopurinol (First Aid Immunology (immunosuppressants and therapeutic antibodies)). COMLEX: febuxostat raises toxicity the same way, and purine-analog benefit in IBD has a median delay of 17 weeks because thioguanine nucleotides accumulate slowly (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 → Obj 2 — differentiating the inflammatory bowel diseases","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"E":"Folate antagonism is a sulfasalazine concern, whereas allopu","A":"Renal excretion clears only small amounts of unchanged azath","B":"Azathioprine is already well absorbed from the gut; allopuri","D":"This is predictable dose-related toxicity, not allergy: with"}},{"id":"sdl38_q17","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Compare the major pharmacologic therapies used to treat ulcerative colitis and Crohn’s disease, including aminosalicylates, glucocorticoids, purine analogs, and anti-tumor necrosis factor therapy with emphasis on their mechanisms of action, therapeutic roles, and key adverse effects.","batch":2,"stem":"A 31-year-old man with fistulizing Crohn disease who emigrated from the Philippines will begin infliximab. Which test must be done first?","choices":{"A":"Latent tuberculosis test","B":"Serum ceruloplasmin level","C":"Hepatitis A antibody titer","D":"Stool culture panel","E":"Repeat colonic biopsy"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Anti-TNF therapy suppresses the TH1 response; all patients need a tuberculin skin test or interferon gamma release assay first, and positive patients get prophylaxis before therapy. Serum ceruloplasmin is low in Wilson disease from ATP7B mutation and is not a screen required before anti-TNF therapy. Anti-TNF therapy risks reactivation of hepatitis B, not hepatitis A, alongside the mandatory tuberculosis screen. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Anti-Tumor Necrosis Factor Therapy). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: TNF is important in granuloma formation and stabilization, and anti-TNF drugs can cause drug-induced lupus (First Aid Immunology (immunosuppressants and therapeutic antibodies)). COMLEX: serious infection risk rises markedly with concurrent corticosteroids (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 → Obj 2 — differentiating the inflammatory bowel diseases","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Serum ceruloplasmin is low in Wilson disease from ATP7B muta","C":"Anti-TNF therapy risks reactivation of hepatitis B, not hepa"}},{"id":"sdl38_q18","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Compare the major pharmacologic therapies used to treat ulcerative colitis and Crohn’s disease, including aminosalicylates, glucocorticoids, purine analogs, and anti-tumor necrosis factor therapy with emphasis on their mechanisms of action, therapeutic roles, and key adverse effects.","batch":2,"stem":"A 24-year-old woman has mild to moderate Crohn disease of the ileum and ascending colon. She wants a glucocorticoid with fewer systemic adverse effects. Which is best?","choices":{"A":"Oral prednisone at high dose","B":"Hydrocortisone retention enema","C":"IV methylprednisolone","D":"Oral dexamethasone daily","E":"Controlled-release budesonide"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Budesonide has high glucocorticoid receptor affinity but extensive CYP3A4 first-pass metabolism; the distal ileum and colon release form treats mild to moderate ileocolonic Crohn disease with less systemic toxicity. High-dose oral prednisone is effective but causes the systemic adverse effects she wants to avoid. Hydrocortisone enemas treat distal colonic flares and cannot reach the ileum and ascending colon. IV methylprednisolone is systemic therapy for severe disease, not a low-systemic option for mild disease. Dexamethasone is a potent systemic glucocorticoid with about 30 times the anti-inflammatory potency of hydrocortisone, so it does not limit systemic effects. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Glucocorticoids). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: glucocorticoids inactivate NF-kB and inhibit synthesis of almost all cytokines (First Aid Immunology (immunosuppressants and therapeutic antibodies)). COMLEX: potent CYP3A4 inhibitors raise plasma levels of the controlled-release steroid severalfold (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 → Obj 2 — differentiating the inflammatory bowel diseases","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"High-dose oral prednisone is effective but causes the system","B":"Hydrocortisone enemas treat distal colonic flares and cannot","C":"IV methylprednisolone is systemic therapy for severe disease","D":"Dexamethasone is a potent systemic glucocorticoid with about"}},{"id":"trial_sdl38_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the mechanisms of action of medications used to treat constipation, including stool softeners, osmotic laxatives, and stimulant laxatives, and explain their clinical applications.","batch":3,"stem":"A 62-year-old woman on lactulose for constipation has cramps and flatus. What causes these effects?","choices":{"A":"Colonic bacterial fermentation","B":"Small-bowel enzyme deficiency","C":"Direct irritation of the mucosa","D":"Enteric nerve stimulation","E":"Gastric acid hypersecretion"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Sorbitol and lactulose are nonabsorbable sugars metabolized by colonic bacteria, producing flatus and cramps; PEG does not. Direct enteric nervous system stimulation is the mechanism of stimulant laxatives such as senna and bisacodyl, not lactulose. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Osmotic Laxatives). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Katzung) — detail not in SDL 38 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: lactulose also treats hepatic encephalopathy by trapping NH4+ in the colon (First Aid Gastrointestinal Pharmacology). COMLEX: high-dose osmotic purgatives evacuate the bowel within 1 to 3 hours (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 5 — osmotic versus secretory diarrhea","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Katzung","whyNot":{"D":"Direct enteric nervous system stimulation is the mechanism o"}},{"id":"trial_sdl38_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the mechanisms of action of medications used to treat constipation, including stool softeners, osmotic laxatives, and stimulant laxatives, and explain their clinical applications.","batch":3,"stem":"A 45-year-old man starts psyllium for mild constipation. How does it act?","choices":{"A":"Surfactant softening of stool","B":"Direct enteric nerve activation","C":"Mu opioid receptor blockade","D":"Water-absorbing bulky gel","E":"Apical chloride channel opening"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Bulk-forming laxatives are indigestible hydrophilic colloids that absorb water to form a bulky gel, distending the colon and promoting peristalsis. Surfactant softening is the action of docusate and glycerin, not bulk-forming fiber. Direct enteric nerve stimulation is how stimulant laxatives such as senna and bisacodyl work. Mu-opioid receptor blockade in the gut is the mechanism of peripheral opioid antagonists such as methylnaltrexone and naloxegol. Opening of the type 2 chloride channel (ClC-2) is the mechanism of lubiprostone. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Bulk-Forming Laxatives). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Katzung) — detail not in SDL 38 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: soluble fibers draw water into the lumen and form a viscous liquid, with bloating as an adverse effect (First Aid Gastrointestinal Pharmacology). COMLEX: polycarbophil is a synthetic bulk-forming fiber alongside psyllium and methylcellulose (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 5 — defecation reflex and anal sphincter control","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Katzung","whyNot":{"A":"Surfactant softening is the action of docusate and glycerin,","B":"Direct enteric nerve stimulation is how stimulant laxatives ","C":"Mu-opioid receptor blockade in the gut is the mechanism of p","E":"Opening of the type 2 chloride channel (ClC-2) is the mechan"}},{"id":"trial_sdl38_q03","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare the mechanisms of action, therapeutic uses, and adverse effects of major classes of antidiarrheal medications, including opioid agonists, bile acid–binding resins, and octreotide, and identify clinical scenarios in which each is indicated.","batch":3,"stem":"A 66-year-old woman on several heart medicines needs a bile acid resin for diarrhea. Which choice has the fewest drug-binding interactions?","choices":{"A":"Colestipol","B":"Sucralfate","C":"Kaolin-pectin","D":"Activated charcoal","E":"Colesevelam"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Colesevelam does not appear to affect absorption of other drugs significantly, unlike older resins that must be separated from other medicines. Colestipol, like cholestyramine, binds coadministered oral medications, which must be given 2 hours apart. Sucralfate is a sucrose–aluminum hydroxide salt that forms a protective paste over ulcers and is not a bile acid resin. Kaolin-pectin is an OTC adsorbent antidiarrheal rather than a bile acid–binding resin. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Bile Salt-Binding Resins). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: bile acid resins lower LDL by interrupting enterohepatic bile acid recycling (First Aid Cardiovascular Pharmacology, lipid-lowering agents). COMLEX: removing more bile acids from a small pool can worsen fat malabsorption (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 3 — enterohepatic circulation and terminal ileal bile salt loss","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Colestipol, like cholestyramine, binds coadministered oral m","B":"Sucralfate is a sucrose–aluminum hydroxide salt that forms a","C":"Kaolin-pectin is an OTC adsorbent antidiarrheal rather than "}},{"id":"trial_sdl38_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare the mechanisms of action, therapeutic uses, and adverse effects of major classes of antidiarrheal medications, including opioid agonists, bile acid–binding resins, and octreotide, and identify clinical scenarios in which each is indicated.","batch":3,"stem":"A 52-year-old woman has scleroderma with intestinal pseudo-obstruction. A somatostatin analog is given at 50 mcg SC. What is its intended effect?","choices":{"A":"Decreased gastric acid output","B":"Increased pancreatic secretion","C":"Increased small-bowel motility","D":"Decreased portal venous flow","E":"Increased hepatic bile flow"},"correct":"C","explanation":"At low doses (50 mcg SC) octreotide stimulates small-bowel motility, used in bacterial overgrowth or scleroderma pseudo-obstruction; higher doses inhibit motility. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Octreotide). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Katzung) — detail not in SDL 38 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: somatostatin analogs are used for acute variceal bleeds and VIPoma (First Aid Gastrointestinal Pharmacology). COMLEX: subcutaneous dosing gives a 6 to 12 hour duration of action (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 4 — secretory diarrhea and cAMP-mediated electrolyte loss","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Katzung"},{"id":"trial_sdl38_q05","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Compare the major pharmacologic therapies used to treat ulcerative colitis and Crohn’s disease, including aminosalicylates, glucocorticoids, purine analogs, and anti-tumor necrosis factor therapy with emphasis on their mechanisms of action, therapeutic roles, and key adverse effects.","batch":3,"stem":"A 38-year-old man's ulcerative colitis flare resolves on prednisone. Why should it not be continued long term?","choices":{"A":"Ineffective for active colitis","B":"Ineffective for maintenance","C":"Rapid loss of oral absorption","D":"Frequent drug-induced lupus","E":"Blocks folate absorption"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Corticosteroids are not useful for maintaining remission; aminosalicylates or immunosuppressants are used instead, and steroids are tapered after a response. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Glucocorticoids). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: glucocorticoids inhibit NF-kB and cause Cushing syndrome and osteoporosis with long-term use (First Aid Immunology (immunosuppressants and therapeutic antibodies)). COMLEX: prednisone induces remission in moderate to severe IBD; oral prednisone is started at 40 to 60 mg/d, and higher doses add toxicity (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 → Obj 2 — differentiating the inflammatory bowel diseases","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl38_q06","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Compare the major pharmacologic therapies used to treat ulcerative colitis and Crohn’s disease, including aminosalicylates, glucocorticoids, purine analogs, and anti-tumor necrosis factor therapy with emphasis on their mechanisms of action, therapeutic roles, and key adverse effects.","batch":3,"stem":"A 27-year-old woman given episodic infliximab for ulcerative colitis loses response and has infusion reactions. What is the likely cause?","choices":{"A":"Rapid hepatic drug clearance","B":"TNF receptor gene mutation","C":"Proteolysis in the gut lumen","D":"Antibodies against the drug","E":"Tolerance at receptor level"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Antibodies to the antibody can blunt response and raise infusion reaction risk; they are more likely with episodic than scheduled dosing and less likely with concurrent immunomodulators. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Anti-Tumor Necrosis Factor Therapy). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: infliximab is a chimeric anti-TNF-alpha monoclonal antibody (First Aid Immunology (immunosuppressants and therapeutic antibodies)). COMLEX: infusion reactions are more common with second or later infusions (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 → Obj 2 — differentiating the inflammatory bowel diseases","isHighYield":true},{"id":"grid_sdl38_q01","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Compare the major pharmacologic therapies used to treat ulcerative colitis and Crohn’s disease, including aminosalicylates, glucocorticoids, purine analogs, and anti-tumor necrosis factor therapy with emphasis on their mechanisms of action, therapeutic roles, and key adverse effects.","batch":4,"stem":"A 31-year-old woman has moderate-to-severe ileocolonic Crohn disease. She has needed 3 courses of prednisone in the past year and has a flare each time the dose is tapered. Her gastroenterologist starts an oral steroid-sparing drug and explains that full benefit may take several months, and that an injectable biologic might later be combined with it partly to prevent antibodies against the biologic. Which of the following sets of features best characterizes the oral drug started?","grid":{"headers":["Disease activity treated","Maintains remission","Marrow suppression"]},"choices":{"A":["Mild–moderate","Yes","Yes"],"B":["Moderate–severe","No","No"],"C":["Moderate–severe","Yes","No"],"D":["Moderate–severe","Yes","Yes"],"E":["Mild–moderate","No","No"]},"correct":"D","explanation":"Choice D (moderate–severe disease, maintains remission, marrow suppression yes) is correct. Azathioprine and 6-mercaptopurine are prodrugs converted to 6-thioguanine nucleotides that inhibit purine synthesis and promote T-cell apoptosis. The notes describe their use to induce and maintain remission in moderate-to-severe UC and Crohn disease, allowing steroids to be reduced, with onset delayed by up to 17 weeks and frequent combination with anti-TNF biologics to suppress anti-drug antibodies. Bone marrow suppression (leukopenia) is the key toxicity and becomes life-threatening with allopurinol. Choice A (mild–moderate disease, maintains remission, myelosuppression yes) is sulfasalazine, first-line for inducing and maintaining remission in mild-to-moderate UC, whose sulfonamide component causes myelosuppression. Choice B (moderate–severe disease, no maintenance, myelosuppression no) is systemic prednisone, which induces rapid remission but does not maintain it and causes adrenal suppression, osteoporosis, and hyperglycemia. Choice C (moderate–severe disease, maintains remission, myelosuppression no) is an anti-TNF antibody such as infliximab, given by infusion or injection for refractory disease; its risks are infusion reactions and latent tuberculosis reactivation. Choice E (mild–moderate disease, no maintenance, myelosuppression no) is controlled-release oral budesonide, used for mild-to-moderate ileal and ascending colon Crohn disease with fewer systemic effects, but it is a glucocorticoid and does not maintain remission. Sources: Exam 3 lecture notes (SDL 38, section 3: aminosalicylates, glucocorticoids, purine analogs, anti-TNF therapy); Katzung Ch. 62 (drugs used in IBD). The absence of myelosuppression for glucocorticoids and anti-TNF agents is inferred because the notes do not list it.","boardPrep":"Step 1: azathioprine/6-MP → 6-thioguanine nucleotides; steroid-sparing maintenance in IBD; leukopenia, hepatotoxicity, pancreatitis; severe leukopenia with allopurinol (xanthine oxidase) (Exam 3 notes, SDL 38). COMLEX: glucocorticoids induce but never maintain IBD remission.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 32 Obj 3 — Crohn disease versus ulcerative colitis","isHighYield":true}],"39":[{"id":"sdl39_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the basic pharmacology, mechanisms of action, metabolism, clinical applications and adverse effects for each of the following classes of chemotherapy: fluoropyrimidines, platinum analogs, epidophyllotoxins and growth factor receptor inhibitors.","batch":1,"stem":"The 5-FU metabolite FdUMP exerts its main cytotoxic effect by binding which enzyme?","choices":{"A":"Dihydrofolate reductase","B":"Ribonucleotide reductase","C":"DNA polymerase alpha","D":"Thymidylate synthase","E":"Thymidine kinase enzyme"},"correct":"D","explanation":"FdUMP forms a covalent ternary complex with thymidylate synthase and 5,10-methylenetetrahydrofolate, blocking de novo thymidylate synthesis (\"thymineless death\"); FUTP and FdUTP are also incorporated into RNA and DNA, and the drug acts in S phase. Dihydrofolate reductase is inhibited by methotrexate; FdUMP binds thymidylate synthase. Ribonucleotide reductase is the target of hydroxyurea, not FdUMP. FdUMP forms a covalent ternary complex with thymidylate synthase and reduced folate; thymidine kinase is not its target. Sources: Katzung Ch. 54 (Fluoropyrimidines). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: 5-FU is a pyrimidine analog bioactivated to 5-FdUMP, lowering dTMP and DNA synthesis, and its effects are enhanced by adding leucovorin (First Aid Hematology and Oncology Pharmacology). COMLEX: 5-FU is given IV because it is broken down rapidly in the GI tract, and its plasma half-life is about 10 to 15 minutes (Katzung Ch. 54).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 4 — colorectal adenocarcinoma","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Dihydrofolate reductase is inhibited by methotrexate; FdUMP ","B":"Ribonucleotide reductase is the target of hydroxyurea, not F","E":"FdUMP forms a covalent ternary complex with thymidylate synthase and r"}},{"id":"sdl39_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the basic pharmacology, mechanisms of action, metabolism, clinical applications and adverse effects for each of the following classes of chemotherapy: fluoropyrimidines, platinum analogs, epidophyllotoxins and growth factor receptor inhibitors.","batch":1,"stem":"A 58-year-old man receiving FOLFOX for stage III colon cancer notices tingling in his fingers and throat when he holds a chilled drink during the infusion. Which toxicity is this?","choices":{"A":"Acute sensory neurotoxicity","B":"Cumulative motor axonopathy","C":"Cryoglobulin vasculitis flare","D":"Hypocalcemic tetany episode","E":"Cerebellar ataxia toxicity"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The platinum agent in FOLFOX causes dose-limiting peripheral sensory neuropathy in two forms: an acute form triggered and worsened by cold, and a chronic cumulative dose-dependent form that is more readily reversible than cisplatin neuropathy. The chronic oxaliplatin neuropathy is cumulative and sensory, whereas cold-triggered symptoms during infusion are the acute form. Cold-triggered sensory symptoms during infusion are the acute form of oxaliplatin neurotoxicity, not a cryoglobulin vasculitis flare. Oxaliplatin neurotoxicity is a peripheral sensory neuropathy whose acute form is triggered by cold, not a cerebellar ataxia. Sources: Katzung Ch. 54 (Platinum Analogs). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: platinum compounds cross-link DNA, are cell cycle nonspecific, and cause peripheral neuropathy, nephrotoxicity, and ototoxicity (First Aid Hematology and Oncology Pharmacology). COMLEX: FOLFOX is the most widely used first-line regimen for metastatic colorectal cancer (Katzung Ch. 54).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 4 — colorectal adenocarcinoma","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"The chronic oxaliplatin neuropathy is cumulative and sensory","C":"Cold-triggered sensory symptoms during infusion are the acut","E":"Oxaliplatin neurotoxicity is a peripheral sensory neuropathy"}},{"id":"sdl39_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the basic pharmacology, mechanisms of action, metabolism, clinical applications and adverse effects for each of the following classes of chemotherapy: fluoropyrimidines, platinum analogs, epidophyllotoxins and growth factor receptor inhibitors.","batch":1,"stem":"Before cetuximab is added for metastatic colorectal cancer, the tumor must show which molecular feature?","choices":{"A":"HER2 gene amplification","B":"Wild-type KRAS and NRAS","C":"High EGFR protein expression","D":"BRAF V600E mutation","E":"Microsatellite instability"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Cetuximab is a chimeric IgG1 antibody against the extracellular domain of EGFR; its efficacy is restricted to tumors with wild-type RAS (KRAS and NRAS), because a mutant RAS signals downstream of the blocked receptor. HER2 amplification selects patients for trastuzumab, not cetuximab. Microsatellite instability-high status selects metastatic colorectal cancer for checkpoint inhibitors such as pembrolizumab, not for cetuximab. Sources: Katzung Ch. 54 (Epidermal Growth Factor Receptor Inhibitors). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: anti-EGFR antibodies cause rash, elevated LFTs, and diarrhea (First Aid Hematology and Oncology Pharmacology). COMLEX: because cetuximab is IgG1, part of its antitumor effect may be immune mediated (Katzung Ch. 54).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 4 — colorectal adenocarcinoma","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"HER2 amplification selects patients for trastuzumab, not cet","E":"Microsatellite instability-high status selects metastatic co"}},{"id":"sdl39_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Recognize the various medication names within each class and appreciate the important differences with each medication within these classes.","batch":1,"stem":"Which orally available prodrug is converted to 5-FU by thymidine phosphorylase within tumor tissue?","choices":{"A":"Gemcitabine","B":"Cytarabine","C":"Methotrexate","D":"Hydroxyurea","E":"Capecitabine"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Capecitabine has 70% to 80% oral bioavailability; liver carboxylesterase and cytidine deaminase form 5'-deoxy-5-fluorouridine, which thymidine phosphorylase (high in colorectal tumors) converts to 5-FU. It is paired with oxaliplatin as CAPOX. Methotrexate is an antifolate that inhibits dihydrofolate reductase and is not converted to 5-FU. Hydroxyurea inhibits ribonucleotide reductase and is not a fluoropyrimidine prodrug. Gemcitabine is a deoxycytidine analog activated by deoxycytidine kinase, not a prodrug converted to 5-FU. Cytarabine is a cytidine analog converted to ara-CTP that inhibits DNA polymerase, not a 5-FU prodrug. Sources: Katzung Ch. 54 (Capecitabine). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: capecitabine is the prodrug of 5-FU listed with the pyrimidine antimetabolites (First Aid Hematology and Oncology Pharmacology). COMLEX: CAPOX is used first line in metastatic disease and as adjuvant therapy for stage III colon cancer (Katzung Ch. 54).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 4 — colorectal adenocarcinoma","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Methotrexate is an antifolate that inhibits dihydrofolate re","D":"Hydroxyurea inhibits ribonucleotide reductase and is not a f","A":"Gemcitabine is a deoxycytidine analog activated by deoxycyti","B":"Cytarabine is a cytidine analog converted to ara-CTP that in"}},{"id":"sdl39_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Recognize the various medication names within each class and appreciate the important differences with each medication within these classes.","batch":1,"stem":"Which DNA cross-linking agent has myelosuppression, with much less renal and GI toxicity than cisplatin, as its main dose-limiting effect?","choices":{"A":"Cisplatin","B":"Ifosfamide","C":"Carboplatin","D":"Cyclophosphamide","E":"Busulfan"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Carboplatin shares the mechanism, resistance, and spectrum of cisplatin, but its dose-limiting toxicity is myelosuppression with significantly less renal and GI toxicity, so it is easier to give and has replaced cisplatin in many regimens. Cisplatin's dose-limiting toxicities are nephrotoxicity and emesis, with minimal myelosuppression. Cyclophosphamide is an alkylating agent whose distinctive toxicities are alopecia and hemorrhagic cystitis, preventable with hydration. Busulfan, used for CML, is associated with skin pigmentation, pulmonary fibrosis and adrenal insufficiency. Ifosfamide is an alkylating agent closely related to cyclophosphamide, notable for hemorrhagic cystitis, not a carboplatin-like platinum analog. Sources: Katzung Ch. 54 (Platinum Analogs). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: platinum compounds cross-link DNA, and cisplatin nephrotoxicity can be prevented with amifostine (First Aid Hematology and Oncology Pharmacology). COMLEX: platinum agents bind mainly the N7 position of guanine and work in all cell cycle phases (Katzung Ch. 54).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 4 — colorectal adenocarcinoma","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Cisplatin's dose-limiting toxicities are nephrotoxicity and ","D":"Cyclophosphamide is an alkylating agent whose distinctive to","E":"Busulfan, used for CML, is associated with skin pigmentation","B":"Ifosfamide is an alkylating agent closely related to cycloph"}},{"id":"sdl39_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Recognize the various medication names within each class and appreciate the important differences with each medication within these classes.","batch":1,"stem":"Which anti-EGFR antibody is a fully human IgG2 with fewer infusion reactions?","choices":{"A":"Panitumumab","B":"Cetuximab","C":"Necitumumab","D":"Trastuzumab","E":"Pertuzumab"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Panitumumab is a fully human IgG2 anti-EGFR antibody, so it is not expected to have immune-mediated effects, and infusion reactions are much less common than with chimeric cetuximab; acneiform rash is a main adverse effect. Cetuximab is a chimeric IgG1 with a higher risk of severe infusion reactions. Necitumumab is a fully human IgG1 for squamous NSCLC, not an IgG2. Trastuzumab is a humanized IgG1 against HER2, not EGFR. Pertuzumab is a humanized IgG1 against HER2 subdomain II, not EGFR. Sources: Katzung Ch. 54 (Epidermal Growth Factor Receptor Inhibitors). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the -umab ending marks a human monoclonal antibody and -ximab a chimeric human-mouse one (First Aid Pharmacology, biologic agents). COMLEX: necitumumab is an IgG1 anti-EGFR antibody used for squamous NSCLC and carries thromboembolic risk (Katzung Ch. 54).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 4 — colorectal adenocarcinoma","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Cetuximab is a chimeric IgG1 with a higher risk of severe in","C":"Necitumumab is a fully human IgG1 for squamous NSCLC, not an","D":"Trastuzumab is a humanized IgG1 against HER2, not EGFR.","E":"Pertuzumab is a humanized IgG1 against HER2 subdomain II, no"}},{"id":"sdl39_q07","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the basic pharmacology, mechanisms of action, metabolism, clinical applications and adverse effects for each of the following classes of chemotherapy: fluoropyrimidines, platinum analogs, epidophyllotoxins and growth factor receptor inhibitors.","batch":2,"stem":"A 61-year-old woman with stage III colon cancer gets her first cycle of an IV fluoropyrimidine with leucovorin and a platinum agent. Within a week she has severe mouth ulcers, profuse diarrhea, and confusion; absolute neutrophil count is 300/µL (reference 1,500–8,000/µL). Deficiency of which enzyme best explains this?","choices":{"A":"Thiopurine methyltransferase","B":"Dihydropyrimidine dehydrogenase","C":"Uridine glucuronosyltransferase","D":"Hypoxanthine phosphoribosyl transferase","E":"Cytidine deaminase enzyme"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Most of an administered 5-FU dose is catabolized by DPD. Partial or complete DPD deficiency, an autosomal recessive trait seen in up to 5% of cancer patients (Katzung Ch. 54 elsewhere cites up to 10% of the North American population), causes severe toxicity with the triad of myelosuppression, GI toxicity (diarrhea, mucositis), and neurotoxicity. TPMT deficiency raises thiopurine toxicity, not 5-FU toxicity. UGT1A1 deficiency increases irinotecan toxicity, not that of the fluoropyrimidine with oxaliplatin. Hypoxanthine-guanine phosphoribosyl transferase activates 6-mercaptopurine to its nucleotide; it does not catabolize 5-FU. Sources: Katzung Ch. 54 (Fluoropyrimidines). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: 5-FU causes myelosuppression and is used for colon and pancreatic cancer (First Aid Hematology and Oncology Pharmacology). COMLEX: not every DPD-deficient patient presents with this classic triad (Katzung Ch. 54).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 4 — colorectal adenocarcinoma","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"TPMT deficiency raises thiopurine toxicity, not 5-FU toxicit","C":"UGT1A1 deficiency increases irinotecan toxicity, not that of","D":"Hypoxanthine-guanine phosphoribosyl transferase activates 6-"}},{"id":"sdl39_q08","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the basic pharmacology, mechanisms of action, metabolism, clinical applications and adverse effects for each of the following classes of chemotherapy: fluoropyrimidines, platinum analogs, epidophyllotoxins and growth factor receptor inhibitors.","batch":2,"stem":"A 66-year-old man receives FOLFIRI for metastatic colon cancer. Two hours into the infusion he has abdominal cramps, watery stools, sweating, and salivation. Which drug best treats this early reaction?","choices":{"A":"Loperamide","B":"Octreotide","C":"Atropine","D":"Ondansetron","E":"Metoclopramide"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Irinotecan causes two forms of diarrhea: an early form within 24 hours that is a cholinergic event effectively treated with atropine, and a late form 2 to 10 days later that may cause severe electrolyte imbalance and dehydration. Loperamide treats the late irinotecan diarrhea, whereas this early cholinergic reaction responds to atropine. Ondansetron is a 5-HT3 antagonist whose action is restricted to vagally mediated and chemotherapy emesis; it does not block muscarinic receptors. Octreotide treats diarrhea from vagotomy, dumping, short-bowel syndrome, or AIDS, not the acute cholinergic syndrome of irinotecan. Metoclopramide is a dopamine D2 antagonist prokinetic that would not reverse cholinergic cramping and salivation. Sources: Katzung Ch. 54 (Camptothecins). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Katzung) — detail not in SDL 39 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: irinotecan and topotecan block topoisomerase I and cause severe myelosuppression and diarrhea (First Aid Hematology and Oncology Pharmacology). COMLEX: irinotecan and its metabolite are cleared mainly in bile and feces, so liver dysfunction requires a dose reduction (Katzung Ch. 54).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 4 — secretory diarrhea and cAMP-mediated electrolyte loss","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Katzung","whyNot":{"A":"Loperamide treats the late irinotecan diarrhea, whereas this","D":"Ondansetron is a 5-HT3 antagonist whose action is restricted","B":"Octreotide treats diarrhea from vagotomy, dumping, short-bow","E":"Metoclopramide is a dopamine D2 antagonist prokinetic that w"}},{"id":"sdl39_q09","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the basic pharmacology, mechanisms of action, metabolism, clinical applications and adverse effects for each of the following classes of chemotherapy: fluoropyrimidines, platinum analogs, epidophyllotoxins and growth factor receptor inhibitors.","batch":2,"stem":"A 24-year-old man with metastatic nonseminomatous testicular cancer receives cisplatin, bleomycin, and a semisynthetic mayapple-root derivative. The derivative acts mainly by which mechanism?","choices":{"A":"Topoisomerase II blockade","B":"Topoisomerase I blockade","C":"Microtubule stabilization","D":"Guanine N7 cross-linking","E":"DNA polymerase blockade"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Etoposide, a semisynthetic podophyllotoxin from the mayapple root, complexes with topoisomerase II and DNA, preventing religation of double-strand breaks and impairing DNA synthesis. It is active in germ cell and lung cancers; renal dysfunction needs dose reduction. Topoisomerase I blockade is the mechanism of irinotecan and topotecan, not etoposide. Microtubule stabilization is the action of taxanes, not the mayapple-derived etoposide. Guanine N7 cross-linking is the mechanism of cisplatin, already in the regimen, not etoposide. DNA polymerase blockade is the action of cytarabine triphosphate (Ara-CTP), not of the mayapple derivative etoposide. Sources: Katzung Ch. 54 (Epipodophyllotoxins). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: topoisomerase II inhibitors cause myelosuppression and alopecia and treat testicular and small cell lung cancer (First Aid Hematology and Oncology Pharmacology). COMLEX: topoisomerase inhibitors arrest cells in the S and G2 phases (First Aid Hematology and Oncology Pharmacology).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 4 — colorectal adenocarcinoma","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Topoisomerase I blockade is the mechanism of irinotecan and ","C":"Microtubule stabilization is the action of taxanes, not the ","D":"Guanine N7 cross-linking is the mechanism of cisplatin, alre","E":"DNA polymerase blockade is the action of cytarabine triphosp"}},{"id":"sdl39_q10","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Recognize the various medication names within each class and appreciate the important differences with each medication within these classes.","batch":2,"stem":"A 59-year-old woman with metastatic colorectal cancer is starting second-line therapy. Her oncologist adds to FOLFIRI a humanized antibody that binds circulating VEGF-A. Which drug was added?","choices":{"A":"Cetuximab","B":"Necitumumab","C":"Trastuzumab","D":"Bevacizumab","E":"Rituximab"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Bevacizumab binds all forms of VEGF-A and keeps it from reaching VEGF receptors; it combines with 5-FU-, irinotecan-, and oxaliplatin-based therapy in metastatic colorectal cancer; unlike the anti-EGFR antibodies, it targets the ligand rather than EGFR. Hypertension, arterial thromboembolism, GI perforation, and proteinuria are key risks. Cetuximab is a chimeric antibody against EGFR, not a humanized anti-VEGF-A antibody. Necitumumab targets EGFR and is approved for squamous NSCLC, not VEGF-A. Trastuzumab targets HER2, not circulating VEGF-A. Rituximab is an anti-CD20 antibody used with CHOP for lymphoma, not an anti-VEGF-A antibody. Sources: Katzung Ch. 54 (Vascular Endothelial Growth Factor). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the anti-VEGF antibody also treats renal cell carcinoma, NSCLC, and angioproliferative retinopathy, with hemorrhage and blood clots as adverse effects (First Aid Hematology and Oncology Pharmacology). COMLEX: it does not appear to worsen the usual toxicities of cytotoxic chemotherapy (Katzung Ch. 54).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 1 — colorectal adenomas and malignant potential","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Cetuximab is a chimeric antibody against EGFR, not a humaniz","B":"Necitumumab targets EGFR and is approved for squamous NSCLC,","C":"Trastuzumab targets HER2, not circulating VEGF-A.","E":"Rituximab is an anti-CD20 antibody used with CHOP for lympho"}},{"id":"sdl39_q11","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Recognize the various medication names within each class and appreciate the important differences with each medication within these classes.","batch":2,"stem":"A 63-year-old man has metastatic colon cancer whose cells lack mismatch repair function and resist the older platinum drugs in culture. Which DNA cross-linking agent is least affected by this resistance?","choices":{"A":"Cisplatin","B":"Ifosfamide","C":"Busulfan","D":"Cyclophosphamide","E":"Oxaliplatin"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Tumors resistant to cisplatin or carboplatin through mismatch repair defects are not cross-resistant to oxaliplatin, which is used with 5-FU and leucovorin (FOLFOX) in metastatic and adjuvant colorectal cancer. Cisplatin is one of the older platinum drugs that mismatch repair-deficient tumors resist. Ifosfamide is a bis(chloroethyl)amine alkylating agent, not the platinum analog that escapes mismatch repair resistance. Busulfan is an alkylating agent used for chronic myeloid leukemia, not the platinum analog that escapes mismatch repair resistance. Cyclophosphamide is a bis(chloroethyl)amine alkylating agent, not the platinum analog that escapes mismatch repair resistance. Sources: Katzung Ch. 54 (Platinum Analogs). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: platinum compounds treat GI, testicular, bladder, ovarian, and lung tumors (First Aid Hematology and Oncology Pharmacology). COMLEX: oxaliplatin regimens are also active in pancreatic, gastroesophageal, and hepatocellular cancer (Katzung Ch. 54).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 3 — hereditary nonpolyposis colorectal cancer and extracolonic cancers","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Cisplatin is one of the older platinum drugs that mismatch r","B":"Ifosfamide is a bis(chloroethyl)amine alkylating agent, not ","C":"Busulfan is an alkylating agent used for chronic myeloid leu","D":"Cyclophosphamide is a bis(chloroethyl)amine alkylating agent"}},{"id":"sdl39_q12","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Recognize the various medication names within each class and appreciate the important differences with each medication within these classes.","batch":2,"stem":"A 57-year-old man on an anti-EGFR antibody plus FOLFOX for metastatic colorectal cancer develops an acneiform facial rash, muscle cramps, and tremor. Which lab abnormality is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Low serum magnesium","B":"High serum potassium","C":"Low serum sodium","D":"High serum calcium","E":"Low serum phosphate"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Hypomagnesemia and acneiform skin rash are the two main adverse effects shared by cetuximab and panitumumab; neuromuscular irritability such as cramps and tremor fits low magnesium. Acneiform rash and hypomagnesemia are the main adverse effects of anti-EGFR antibodies; hyperkalemia is not. Acneiform rash and hypomagnesemia are the main adverse effects of anti-EGFR antibodies; hyponatremia is not. Acneiform rash and hypomagnesemia are the main adverse effects of anti-EGFR antibodies; low phosphate is not. Sources: Katzung Ch. 54 (Epidermal Growth Factor Receptor Inhibitors). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: anti-EGFR antibodies treat metastatic colorectal cancer and head and neck cancer (First Aid Hematology and Oncology Pharmacology). COMLEX: cetuximab given with radiation for head and neck cancer carries a small (1%) sudden-death risk and a black-box warning (Katzung Ch. 54).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 4 — colorectal adenocarcinoma","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Acneiform rash and hypomagnesemia are the main adverse effec","C":"Acneiform rash and hypomagnesemia are the main adverse effects of anti-EGFR antibodies; hypo","E":"Acneiform rash and hypomagnesemia are the main adverse effects of anti-EGFR antibodies; l"}},{"id":"trial_sdl39_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the basic pharmacology, mechanisms of action, metabolism, clinical applications and adverse effects for each of the following classes of chemotherapy: fluoropyrimidines, platinum analogs, epidophyllotoxins and growth factor receptor inhibitors.","batch":3,"stem":"A 55-year-old woman on an oral fluoropyrimidine for colon cancer has red, painful, peeling palms and soles. What is this reaction?","choices":{"A":"Acneiform drug eruption","B":"Fixed drug eruption","C":"Contact dermatitis","D":"Hand-foot syndrome","E":"Erythema multiforme"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Diarrhea and hand-foot syndrome are the main toxicities of capecitabine; the 5-FU it forms causes the same palmar-plantar reaction. Sources: Katzung Ch. 54 (Capecitabine). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: 5-FU adverse effects include myelosuppression and palmar-plantar erythrodysesthesia (First Aid Hematology and Oncology Pharmacology). COMLEX: myelosuppression, mucositis, and alopecia are less common with the oral prodrug than with IV 5-FU (Katzung Ch. 54).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 4 — colorectal adenocarcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl39_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the basic pharmacology, mechanisms of action, metabolism, clinical applications and adverse effects for each of the following classes of chemotherapy: fluoropyrimidines, platinum analogs, epidophyllotoxins and growth factor receptor inhibitors.","batch":3,"stem":"A 64-year-old man with metastatic colorectal cancer receiving an anti-VEGF-A antibody needs elective hernia repair. Why is surgery delayed after stopping the drug?","choices":{"A":"Prolonged neuromuscular block","B":"Impaired wound healing","C":"Raised anesthetic drug levels","D":"Adrenal insufficiency risk","E":"Reduced platelet production"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The main safety concerns with anti-VEGF-A therapy include hypertension, arterial thromboembolic events, wound healing complications, GI perforation, and proteinuria. Sources: Katzung Ch. 54 (Vascular Endothelial Growth Factor). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: anti-VEGF antibody therapy causes hemorrhage and blood clots (First Aid Hematology and Oncology Pharmacology). COMLEX: postoperative chemotherapy usually begins 4 to 6 weeks after surgery to let the incision heal (Katzung Ch. 54).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 4 — colorectal adenocarcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl39_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the basic pharmacology, mechanisms of action, metabolism, clinical applications and adverse effects for each of the following classes of chemotherapy: fluoropyrimidines, platinum analogs, epidophyllotoxins and growth factor receptor inhibitors.","batch":3,"stem":"A 70-year-old woman starts FOLFIRI. Which enzyme converts irinotecan to its 1000-fold more potent active metabolite?","choices":{"A":"Carboxylesterase","B":"Cytidine deaminase","C":"Thymidine phosphorylase","D":"Xanthine oxidase","E":"Monoamine oxidase"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Irinotecan is a prodrug converted mainly in the liver by carboxylesterase to SN-38, a topoisomerase I poison about 1000-fold more potent than the parent drug. Cytidine deaminase converts a capecitabine intermediate to 5′-deoxy-5-fluorouridine, mainly in the liver. Thymidine phosphorylase performs the final intratumoral step converting capecitabine to 5-FU. Xanthine oxidase inactivates 6-mercaptopurine to 6-thiouric acid. Sources: Katzung Ch. 54 (Camptothecins). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Katzung) — detail not in SDL 39 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the -tecan ending marks a topoisomerase I inhibitor (First Aid Hematology and Oncology Pharmacology). COMLEX: myelosuppression and diarrhea are the two most common adverse events of FOLFIRI's camptothecin (Katzung Ch. 54).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 4 — colorectal adenocarcinoma","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Katzung","whyNot":{"B":"Cytidine deaminase converts a capecitabine intermediate to 5","C":"Thymidine phosphorylase performs the final intratumoral step","D":"Xanthine oxidase inactivates 6-mercaptopurine to 6-thiouric "}},{"id":"trial_sdl39_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Recognize the various medication names within each class and appreciate the important differences with each medication within these classes.","batch":3,"stem":"A 31-year-old man with small cell lung cancer is prescribed an epipodophyllotoxin whose oral dose is about twice its IV dose. Which drug is this?","choices":{"A":"Irinotecan","B":"Topotecan","C":"Etoposide","D":"Vincristine","E":"Paclitaxel"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Etoposide has IV and oral forms; oral bioavailability is about 50%, so oral dosing is twice IV dosing, and 30% to 50% is excreted in urine. Irinotecan is a camptothecin topoisomerase I inhibitor prodrug, not an epipodophyllotoxin. Topotecan is a camptothecin topoisomerase I inhibitor, not an epipodophyllotoxin. Vincristine is an M phase–specific vinca alkaloid, not an epipodophyllotoxin. Paclitaxel is an M phase–specific taxane, not an epipodophyllotoxin. Sources: Katzung Ch. 54 (Epipodophyllotoxins). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the epipodophyllotoxins treat leukemia and lymphoma as well as testicular and small cell lung cancer (First Aid Hematology and Oncology Pharmacology). COMLEX: teniposide is the related epipodophyllotoxin listed with it (First Aid Hematology and Oncology Pharmacology).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 4 — colorectal adenocarcinoma","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Irinotecan is a camptothecin topoisomerase I inhibitor prodr","B":"Topotecan is a camptothecin topoisomerase I inhibitor, not a","D":"Vincristine is an M phase–specific vinca alkaloid, not an ep","E":"Paclitaxel is an M phase–specific taxane, not an epipodophyl"}},{"id":"trial_sdl39_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Recognize the various medication names within each class and appreciate the important differences with each medication within these classes.","batch":3,"stem":"A 60-year-old woman whose metastatic colorectal cancer progressed on first-line therapy starts an IgG1 antibody that targets VEGFR-2 itself rather than a VEGF ligand. Which drug is this?","choices":{"A":"Ramucirumab","B":"Ziv-aflibercept","C":"Cetuximab","D":"Regorafenib","E":"Pembrolizumab"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Ramucirumab is an IgG1 antibody against VEGFR-2, the main receptor mediating tumor angiogenesis; ziv-aflibercept is a decoy fusion protein that traps VEGF-A, VEGF-B, and PlGF. Cetuximab is a chimeric IgG1 antibody against the EGFR extracellular domain, not VEGFR-2. Regorafenib is a small-molecule tyrosine kinase inhibitor, not an antibody. Pembrolizumab is an immune checkpoint inhibitor that targets the PD-1 receptor on T cells. Sources: Katzung Ch. 54 (Vascular Endothelial Growth Factor). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: monoclonal antibodies (-mab) target overexpressed cell surface receptors, while -ib small molecules act inside cells (First Aid Pharmacology, drug names). COMLEX: anti-VEGF agents raise blood pressure and the risk of GI perforation (Katzung Ch. 54).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 4 — colorectal adenocarcinoma","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Cetuximab is a chimeric IgG1 antibody against the EGFR extra","D":"Regorafenib is a small-molecule tyrosine kinase inhibitor, n","E":"Pembrolizumab is an immune checkpoint inhibitor that targets"}},{"id":"trial_sdl39_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Recognize the various medication names within each class and appreciate the important differences with each medication within these classes.","batch":3,"stem":"A 68-year-old man whose metastatic colorectal cancer progressed on 5-FU starts an oral combination in which one component blocks breakdown of the other. Which combination is this?","choices":{"A":"Leucovorin-fluorouracil","B":"Irinotecan-cetuximab","C":"Gemcitabine-cisplatin","D":"Cytarabine-daunorubicin","E":"Trifluridine-tipiracil"},"correct":"E","explanation":"TAS-102 combines trifluridine, a weak TS inhibitor incorporated into DNA, with tipiracil, which inhibits thymidine phosphorylase so trifluridine levels stay higher; it works in 5-FU-resistant and RAS-mutant tumors, with neutropenia as the main dose-limiting toxicity. Leucovorin is a reduced folate that stabilizes the FdUMP–thymidylate synthase complex rather than blocking 5-FU breakdown. Cetuximab is a monoclonal antibody against EGFR, so the irinotecan-cetuximab pair is not an oral combination. Sources: Katzung Ch. 54 (Fluoropyrimidines). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: pyrimidine analogs such as 5-FU and cytarabine interfere with DNA synthesis (First Aid Hematology and Oncology Pharmacology). COMLEX: TAS-102 also causes diarrhea, nausea, fatigue, and anorexia (Katzung Ch. 54).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 4 — colorectal adenocarcinoma","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Leucovorin is a reduced folate that stabilizes the FdUMP–thy","B":"Cetuximab is a monoclonal antibody against EGFR, so the irin"}},{"id":"grid_sdl39_q01","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Recognize the various medication names within each class and appreciate the important differences with each medication within these classes.","batch":4,"stem":"A 63-year-old man with resected stage III colon cancer begins adjuvant chemotherapy with intravenous oxaliplatin every 3 weeks plus a tablet he takes twice daily. Six weeks later the skin of his palms and soles is red, painful, and peeling. He has no mouth ulcers, and his neutrophil count is within the reference range. Which of the following sets of features best characterizes the oral drug most likely responsible?","grid":{"headers":["Primary action","Route","Key toxicity"]},"choices":{"A":["TS inhibition","Oral","Hand-foot syndrome"],"B":["TS inhibition","IV","Mucositis, cytopenias"],"C":["Incorporation into DNA","Oral","Neutropenia"],"D":["HER2 kinase inhibition","Oral","Hand-foot syndrome"],"E":["DNA cross-linking","IV","Sensory neuropathy"]},"correct":"A","explanation":"Choice A (TS inhibition, oral, hand-foot syndrome) is correct. Capecitabine is an oral fluoropyrimidine carbamate prodrug converted to 5-FU in the tumor; the last step uses thymidine phosphorylase, which is overexpressed in solid tumors. Its metabolite FdUMP forms a ternary complex with thymidylate synthase and methylenetetrahydrofolate, blocking thymidylate synthesis. The notes say it causes less myelosuppression and mucositis than intravenous 5-FU but a markedly higher incidence of hand-foot syndrome, and it pairs with oxaliplatin in CAPOX. Choice B (TS inhibition, IV, mucositis and cytopenias) is 5-fluorouracil, which must be given intravenously because DPD degrades it rapidly and which causes high rates of mucositis, diarrhea, and myelosuppression. Choice C (incorporation into DNA, oral, neutropenia) is TAS-102: trifluridine is incorporated into DNA, tipiracil blocks its breakdown, and neutropenia rather than hand-foot syndrome is dose-limiting. Choice D (HER2 kinase inhibition, oral, hand-foot syndrome) is an oral HER2 tyrosine kinase inhibitor such as lapatinib or tucatinib, which causes severe diarrhea and hand-foot syndrome but is not a CRC adjuvant fluoropyrimidine. Choice E (DNA cross-linking, IV, sensory neuropathy) is oxaliplatin, the intravenous partner drug, whose dose-limiting toxicity is peripheral sensory neuropathy with cold-induced dysesthesia, not palmar-plantar erythrodysesthesia. Sources: Exam 3 lecture notes (SDL 39, sections 1 and 2: fluoropyrimidines, platinum analogs, growth factor receptor inhibitors, and intra-class distinctions); Katzung Ch. 54 (cancer chemotherapy).","boardPrep":"Step 1: capecitabine = oral 5-FU prodrug activated by thymidine phosphorylase in tumor; more hand-foot syndrome, less myelosuppression and mucositis than IV 5-FU; DPD deficiency causes severe toxicity with either drug (Exam 3 notes, SDL 39). COMLEX: oxaliplatin → cold-induced neuropathy; TAS-102 → neutropenia.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 33 Obj 3 — colorectal cancer pathways and staging context","isHighYield":true}]};
